Bolsonaro also made a conspicuous pre-invasion trip to Moscow, voiced neutrality instead of stronger condemnation, and repeatedly chose caution over leadership at the UN. That supports a midpoint score: better than acquiescence, but not strong enough to count as robust peace leadership.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A score of 4 is too low because Brazil did support diplomacy, humanitarian protection, and UN accountability positions on Ukraine.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A score of 6 is too high because the record remains hedged, with neutrality language, abstentions, and a questionable Moscow signal before the invasion.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Mixed restraint: some humanitarian and legal positioning, but undermined by neutrality and a risky pre-invasion Moscow trip.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:CHN:15832\",\"iso3\":\"CHN\",\"ruler_id\":\"1432\",\"ruler_year_id\":15832,\"ruler_name\":\"Xi Jinping\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"2B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_2b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:2B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:JPN:16638\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:ETH:16782\"],\"score_1_to_10\":3.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":86,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":2.5,\"upper\":4.0},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0018\",\"explanation\":\"Xi publicly said the Ukraine crisis was not something China wanted to see and that countries should not come to the battlefield.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0019\",\"explanation\":\"China said it would support Russia and Ukraine resolving the issue through negotiations and reiterated sovereignty language.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0014\",\"explanation\":\"China's First Committee statement repeated no-first-use and minimum-security language, which is relevant but mostly declaratory.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0007\",\"explanation\":\"China launched major drills and fired missiles near Taiwan, sharply increasing the risk of miscalculation.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0025\",\"explanation\":\"The large-scale drills around Taiwan were a major coercive demonstration, even if conventional rather than nuclear.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0026\",\"explanation\":\"China suspended senior military dialogue and other cooperation with the United States in retaliation for Pelosi's visit.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"China entered 2022 with centralized party-state control over both deterrence rhetoric and Taiwan policy, so the question is whether Xi's conduct constrained escalation or used coercion as leverage.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"The most consequential items are attributable to Xi's governing system and his direct statements, even when operational actions are reported through the military or party spokespeople.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"2B.1 declaratory diplomacy\",\"2B.3 selective restraint language\",\"2B.7 limited negotiation posture\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"2B.2 coercive escalation\",\"2B.4 civilian protection under crisis pressure\",\"2B.5 proxy/force restraint\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0014\",\"explanation\":\"No-first-use rhetoric and minimum-force language partially mitigate the picture, but they are declaratory rather than operational.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0018\",\"explanation\":\"Peace language on Ukraine did not stop later coercive behavior around Taiwan.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Primary Chinese official statements, UN records, and Reuters reporting are combined here; local priors were unavailable in this experiment.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; judgment relies on the embedded cited projections and counterevidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Xi Jinping's 2022 record contains peace language, but the year was dominated by coercive security behavior around Taiwan. China issued declaratory support for negotiation and no-first-use principles, yet the most concrete conduct was large drills, missile fire near Taiwan, and a punitive suspension of military dialogue with the United States. That is not full-scale war, but it is clearly below restraint-oriented leadership and sits in the severe escalation band.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A score of 2 is too low because the dossier includes some declaratory restraint and negotiation language rather than pure war-making with no peace posture at all.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A score of 4 is too high because the operational record is coercive and escalatory, with major Taiwan drills and channel suspension outweighing the rhetoric.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Peace rhetoric persisted, but the year trended toward coercive Taiwan signaling and military-channel disruption.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:COD:16113\",\"iso3\":\"COD\",\"ruler_id\":\"2830\",\"ruler_year_id\":16113,\"ruler_name\":\"Félix Tshisekedi\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"2B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_2b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:2B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:JPN:16638\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:ETH:16782\"],\"score_1_to_10\":5.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":72,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":4.5,\"upper\":6.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0011\",\"explanation\":\"Tshisekedi reiterated an unconditional call for armed groups to join the peace process and condemned M23's breach of the republican commitment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0014\",\"explanation\":\"He set ceasefire and withdrawal conditions for M23 in the Nairobi track, showing an active peace-process stance.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"The Luanda mini-summit imposed a ceasefire, withdrawal, and disarmament framework that Tshisekedi joined.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0012\",\"explanation\":\"The UN experts said the federal government and allies had reasonable grounds for crimes-against-humanity findings and used starvation as a method of warfare in Tigray.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0027\",\"explanation\":\"The defense council's accusatory communique, while active, shows a hard-edged security response rather than clean restraint.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0028\",\"explanation\":\"Tshisekedi's own address described the crisis as aggression backed by Rwanda, but the self-justifying frame leaves the causal picture contested.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"The DRC entered 2022 with a severe inherited eastern war and weak state control over armed groups, so the score turns on whether Tshisekedi improved peace prospects and civilian protection within those limits.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Attribution is mixed but real: Tshisekedi chaired security decisions and peace-process steps, while many harms were inflicted by rebel, regional, or neighboring actors rather than directly by him.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"2B.1 peace-process leadership\",\"2B.7 ceasefire and negotiation\",\"2B.10 incremental stabilization\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"2B.4 civilian-harm control\",\"2B.5 proxy/ally restraint\",\"2B.9 accountability for abuses\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0024\",\"explanation\":\"Humanitarian crisis reports show the civilian emergency was still severe and not fully controlled.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0020\",\"explanation\":\"Rights reporting tied abuses in Western Tigray to federal acquiescence or possible participation, which cuts against a clean restraint reading.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Official presidium statements, UN humanitarian records, rights reports, and local reporting are mixed in the dossier; local priors were unavailable in this experiment.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judgment follows the embedded cited evidence and dossier-local counterevidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Félix Tshisekedi's 2022 record is mixed but not purely negative. He did pursue peace-process steps, drove ceasefire and withdrawal language, and participated in late-year regional efforts that improved humanitarian access, but those gains were set against an eastern war marked by mass displacement, severe abuses, and disputed responsibility involving both rebels and state actors. The result is a middle score: better than passive failure, but not strong enough to treat the year as clearly peace-consolidating.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A score of 5.0 is too low because the dossier contains real peace-process leadership, ceasefire framing, and late-year access improvements.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A score of 6.5 is too high because civilian harm, contested attribution, and incomplete implementation remain substantial, and the peace gains were partial rather than settled.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"From crisis management toward a late peace pivot, but the year still closed with unresolved violence and attribution disputes.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"iso3\":\"DEU\",\"ruler_id\":\"2975\",\"ruler_year_id\":16984,\"ruler_name\":\"Olaf Scholz\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"2B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_2b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:2B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:JPN:16638\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:ETH:16782\"],\"score_1_to_10\":7.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":88,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":7.0,\"upper\":8.0},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0014\",\"explanation\":\"Scholz condemned Russia's attack as unjustified aggression and said Germany had pursued diplomacy before the invasion.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0017\",\"explanation\":\"He urged Putin to accept a ceasefire to improve humanitarian conditions and open a diplomatic path.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0019\",\"explanation\":\"Germany joined the ICJ and accountability line, supporting lawful settlement and compliance with international rulings.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0024\",\"explanation\":\"Scholz reiterated that Russia's war had no justification and framed continued support as tied to the UN order.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0015\",\"explanation\":\"Germany shifted to supplying defensive weapons, a necessary but still militarily involved response.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0022\",\"explanation\":\"Scholz said Germany would continue sending weapons for as long as Ukraine needed them, which sustained the war effort.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0025\",\"explanation\":\"Year-end policy still coupled peace language with military support, so the record is restrained but not pacific.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Germany inherited a major external war on its eastern flank and had to balance deterrence, allied solidarity, and humanitarian pressure against Russia.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"The strongest evidence is directly Scholz's own speech and government decisions, with only minor reliance on media summaries.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"2B.1 diplomacy-first response\",\"2B.7 ceasefire and legal accountability\",\"2B.10 more lawful regional order\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"2B.2 war initiation\",\"2B.5 proxy conduct\",\"2B.8 militarization concerns\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0022\",\"explanation\":\"The arms-for-Ukraine posture is lawful and defensive but keeps Germany deeply involved in the conflict.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0025\",\"explanation\":\"The year-end speech made clear that military support continued alongside diplomacy, limiting the restraint ceiling.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Official federal speeches and foreign-office statements are triangulated with Reuters reporting; local priors were unavailable in this experiment.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judgment follows the embedded dossier evidence and its counterevidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Olaf Scholz's 2022 record is strongly peace-oriented by Chapter 2B standards. Germany condemned Russia's invasion, pushed ceasefire and legal accountability language, and anchored its support for Ukraine in the UN order, yet it also moved into a substantial military-aid posture and sustained that support through the year. That is a restrained and lawful security response, but not an exceptional one, because it combines diplomacy with real wartime involvement.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A score of 7.0 is too low because the record includes repeated explicit diplomacy, legal accountability, and ceasefire efforts from the chancellery itself.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A score of 8.0 is too high because Germany's large military support and continued wartime role prevent the dossier from reading as unusually pure restraint.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Defensive and legally framed, with deliberate militarization in support of Ukraine rather than direct aggression.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:EGY:16232\",\"iso3\":\"EGY\",\"ruler_id\":\"2851\",\"ruler_year_id\":16232,\"ruler_name\":\"Abdel Fattah el-Sisi\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"2B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_2b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:2B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:JPN:16638\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:ETH:16782\"],\"score_1_to_10\":7.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":75,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":6.5,\"upper\":7.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0015\",\"explanation\":\"UN reporting credited Egypt with a crucial role in brokering the Gaza ceasefire, preventing a broader war.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0021\",\"explanation\":\"Egypt hosted Libya's constitutional-track talks under UN auspices, showing active facilitation of a political process.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0024\",\"explanation\":\"Sisi said Egypt would support political, security, and economic stability in Sudan, signaling regional stabilization rather than coercion.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0019\",\"explanation\":\"Egypt voted for the Ukraine resolution and argued that hostilities should stop and diplomacy should prevail.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0022\",\"explanation\":\"The Libya talks ended without agreement, so Egypt's facilitation was real but not outcome-complete.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0025\",\"explanation\":\"The GERD dispute shows hard-edged regional pressure and security framing, not purely soft diplomacy.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Egypt's baseline was a regional mediator with strong security interests and limited leverage over war outcomes in Gaza, Libya, Sudan, and Ukraine.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"The evidence is mostly state-level and mediation-oriented, but it is attributable to Sisi's government and direct presidential statements where available.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"2B.1 mediation and de-escalation\",\"2B.7 regional peace facilitation\",\"2B.10 more stable neighborhood relations\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"2B.2 war initiation\",\"2B.4 battlefield conduct\",\"2B.5 proxy restraint\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0022\",\"explanation\":\"Egypt's mediation often produced process without final settlement, which limits the score ceiling.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0025\",\"explanation\":\"The GERD dispute shows coercive regional pressure remained part of the posture.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"UN records, official Egyptian statements, and local reporting are the main sources; local priors were unavailable in this experiment.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judgment rests on the embedded dossier and cited counterevidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Abdel Fattah el-Sisi's 2022 international conduct is mediation-heavy and generally lawful. Egypt helped broker a Gaza ceasefire, hosted Libya talks, pushed Sudan stability language, and supported diplomatic settlement framing on Ukraine, but those efforts were only partly successful and often remained process-oriented rather than outcome-determinative. The score therefore lands in the positive middle range: strong on restraint and facilitation, but not exceptional because the record shows limited proof of decisive, durable settlement.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A score of 6.0 is too low because the dossier contains multiple concrete mediation and stabilization actions, not just neutral rhetoric.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A score of 7.5 is too high because several initiatives did not produce durable outcomes and some regional pressure tactics remained part of the record.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Stability-first and mediation-heavy, but with partial outcomes and unresolved regional security pressure.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:ETH:16782\",\"iso3\":\"ETH\",\"ruler_id\":\"2310\",\"ruler_year_id\":16782,\"ruler_name\":\"Abiy Ahmed\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"2B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_2b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:2B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:JPN:16638\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:ETH:16782\"],\"score_1_to_10\":4.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":76,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":3.5,\"upper\":5.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0008\",\"explanation\":\"The government declared an indefinite humanitarian truce to save lives and facilitate aid delivery.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0015\",\"explanation\":\"The UN welcomed the truce and urged a long-term ceasefire, confirming this was a real de-escalation step.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0017\",\"explanation\":\"The later permanent cessation of hostilities agreement and Abiy's commitment to implementation were the year's strongest peace pivot.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0025\",\"explanation\":\"UN humanitarian partners reported federal approval of resumed flights and operations, showing improved access after the peace pivot.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0012\",\"explanation\":\"UN experts found reasonable grounds to believe the federal government and allies committed crimes against humanity and used starvation as a method of warfare.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0020\",\"explanation\":\"The HRW/Amnesty report tied serious abuses in Western Tigray to acquiescence and possible federal participation.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0021\",\"explanation\":\"The UN Secretary-General said the situation was spiraling out of control and civilians were paying a horrific price.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Abiy inherited a severe internal war rather than an external interstate conflict, so the relevant question is whether he reduced violence and restored lawful order within that crisis.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Attribution is mixed because many harms were carried out by federal allies or regional forces, but the truce, peace agreement, and implementation steps were still under Abiy's government authority.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"2B.1 de-escalation and ceasefire\",\"2B.7 negotiated settlement\",\"2B.10 partial stabilization\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"2B.4 civilian protection\",\"2B.5 proxy restraint\",\"2B.9 accountability for abuses\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0012\",\"explanation\":\"Atrocity findings in the same year sharply limit any claim of a clean peace record.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0021\",\"explanation\":\"The UN's 'no military solution' warning shows the conflict was still highly dangerous and unresolved.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"UN, AU, humanitarian, and local reporting are combined with some official Ethiopian statements; local priors were unavailable in this experiment.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judgment uses the embedded dossier, especially the late-year peace pivot and the contrary atrocity record.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Abiy Ahmed's 2022 international-security record is mixed in a serious way. The year began with a humanitarian truce and ended with a cessation-of-hostilities agreement plus better humanitarian access, which are real peace achievements, but they sit beside grave atrocity allegations, starvation allegations, and a conflict environment that remained catastrophic for much of the year. The score therefore sits below the positive range, reflecting meaningful late-year restraint but not enough to overcome the severity of the year's violence.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A score of 3.5 is too low because the dossier includes genuine ceasefire, peace agreement, and access-improvement evidence.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A score of 5.5 is too high because the atrocities and civilian-harm findings remain severe and substantially undermine a restraint reading.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"From truce to peace agreement, but against a backdrop of grave civilian harm and disputed federal responsibility.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IDN:16199\",\"iso3\":\"IDN\",\"ruler_id\":\"2636\",\"ruler_year_id\":16199,\"ruler_name\":\"Joko Widodo\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"2B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_2b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:2B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:JPN:16638\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:ETH:16782\"],\"score_1_to_10\":7.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":84,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":7.0,\"upper\":8.0},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0018\",\"explanation\":\"Jokowi's Kyiv statement emphasized sovereignty, territorial integrity, humanitarian aid, and a peace bridge role.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0019\",\"explanation\":\"He said peace and humanitarian issues were Indonesia's foreign-policy priority and that dialogue should remain open.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0026\",\"explanation\":\"Jokowi framed the G20 declaration on Ukraine as a major success of Indonesia's presidency, with the war condemned as a breach of borders and integrity.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0027\",\"explanation\":\"Reuters corroborated that the G20 declaration condemned the war and said the threat or use of nuclear weapons was inadmissible.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0023\",\"explanation\":\"Indonesia's Bucha-related abstention shows caution and process concerns rather than maximal pressure.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0024\",\"explanation\":\"The later annexation vote was supportive but still mostly legal-diplomatic, not a demonstrated conflict-ending intervention.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Indonesia was not a belligerent and had limited leverage, so the score turns on whether Jokowi used his G20 platform to produce lawful, peace-oriented diplomatic outcomes.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"The clearest items are directly Jokowi's speeches and official presidency transcripts, with multilateral outcomes credited only insofar as his leadership helped shape them.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"2B.1 diplomacy and mediation\",\"2B.7 dialogue and summit leadership\",\"2B.10 more lawful regional/international relations\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"2B.2 war initiation\",\"2B.4 civilian harm\",\"2B.5 proxy restraint\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0023\",\"explanation\":\"Abstention on the Bucha-related measure shows some caution rather than maximal anti-aggression pressure.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0024\",\"explanation\":\"Much of the strongest evidence is multilateral, so direct personal leverage is limited.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Official Indonesian transcripts, UN records, and Reuters corroboration provide the strongest evidence; local priors were unavailable in this experiment.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judgment is based on the embedded chapter evidence and counterevidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Joko Widodo's 2022 record is among the stronger peace-oriented dossiers in the batch. He used his international platform to urge peace, carry messages between leaders, stress sovereignty and humanitarian relief, and help produce a G20 declaration that condemned the war and rejected nuclear threats. The main limitation is that much of this was multilateral or rhetorical rather than a personally enforced settlement, so the score is high but not perfect.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A score of 7.0 is too low because Jokowi produced genuine peace-diplomacy outcomes, not just generic non-aggression language.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A score of 8.0 is too high because the record is still mostly summit diplomacy and legal positioning, with limited direct leverage over the war itself.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Highly peace-oriented multilateral leadership, with the G20 used to press a lawful anti-war line.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\",\"iso3\":\"IND\",\"ruler_id\":\"1517\",\"ruler_year_id\":19645,\"ruler_name\":\"Narendra Modi\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"2B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_2b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:2B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:JPN:16638\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:ETH:16782\"],\"score_1_to_10\":6.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":81,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":5.5,\"upper\":6.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0016\",\"explanation\":\"Modi told Zelenskyy India stood for peaceful resolution and direct dialogue.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0018\",\"explanation\":\"He said he had appealed to both Putin and Zelenskyy for peace and condemned the Bucha killings.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0020\",\"explanation\":\"India reiterated concern about Ukraine and called for an immediate cessation of violence and direct diplomacy.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0023\",\"explanation\":\"India and China continued military and diplomatic dialogue on the LAC, showing border-management restraint.\"}],\"decisive_