{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"schema_version\":\"ruler_chapter_judgment_v1\",\"job_key\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"run_key\":\"2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1\",\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"target_year\":2022,\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"evaluations\":[{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:BGD:17068\",\"iso3\":\"BGD\",\"ruler_id\":\"2992\",\"ruler_year_id\":17068,\"ruler_name\":\"Sheikh Hasina\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\"],\"score_1_to_10\":3.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":83,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":3.0,\"upper\":4.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0023\",\"explanation\":\"Concentrated power, unlawful killings, disappearances, torture, arbitrary detention, and poor accountability were reported as major 2022 problems under Hasina's government.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0024\",\"explanation\":\"Mass arrests and police raids against the opposition in late 2022 showed recurring repression with real domestic-safety consequences.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"Military detention and death in the Chittagong Hill Tracts showed abuse by state forces under the government environment she controlled.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0035\",\"explanation\":\"A limited evidence-law reform for sexual-violence complainants shows some remedial movement, even if narrow.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"Election-language about all parties contesting was permissive rhetoric, but it did not itself create protection on the ground.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"A rally eventually ending peacefully after last-minute permission is a restraint signal, though only partial.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Bangladesh already had a coercive security and accountability environment; the score reflects Hasina's responsibility for maintaining and tolerating it in 2022, not a claim that every abuse was personally ordered by her.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mostly state and security-apparatus conduct under Hasina's government, with only some direct personal statements and cabinet actions.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.4\",\"3B.7\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.3\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.6\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0035\",\"explanation\":\"Partial legal reform in a sexual-violence evidence rule points to some remedial capacity.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"The peaceful end of the rally weakens any claim of totalized domestic terror.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"Permissive election rhetoric is a weak restraint signal, not proof of actual safety.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Mostly official human-rights reporting, NGO monitoring, and Bangladeshi/Reuters-style contemporaneous reporting; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the score rests on the cited projection evidence and the embedded contrary notes.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"This is a low domestic-safety score, but not the historical floor. The record shows recurring state and state-tolerated abuse: concentrated executive power, major human-rights violations, opposition raids and mass arrests, military custody abuse, and weak accountability. At the same time, there is some evidence of limited reform and occasional restraint, including a narrow evidence-law amendment and a peaceful rally after permission was restored. Those are real but modest counterweights. The evidence supports a score in the severe-coercion band rather than a materially higher score because the year still features repeated repression and impunity, but not the kind of fully generalized mass-terror system that would justify an even lower anchor.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 1-2 because the dossier shows severe recurring repression, but not the mass-violence or systematic-terror floor required for the absolute bottom of the scale.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 5+ because the reforms are narrow and the repression record in 2022 remains broad, recurring, and only weakly constrained.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"recurring\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"state-aligned and tolerated\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"weak; some narrow reform, but impunity remains the dominant pattern\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"mixed but constrained; criticism existed, yet protest and opposition activity faced retaliation\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"some limited reform signals, but the end-state in 2022 remains substantially repressive\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"high formal authority; many harms are institutional rather than proven personal orders\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.3 incitement and 3B.5 emergency-power misuse are weaker than the protest, detention, and minority-protection record\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:BRA:19646\",\"iso3\":\"BRA\",\"ruler_id\":\"3413\",\"ruler_year_id\":19646,\"ruler_name\":\"Jair Bolsonaro\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\"],\"score_1_to_10\":4.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":78,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":4.0,\"upper\":5.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0028\",\"explanation\":\"Bolsonaro's anti-human-rights campaign discourse and attacks on institutions helped create an intimidatory climate.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0034\",\"explanation\":\"Undermining Indigenous protection, land recognition, and environmental agencies materially weakened safety for Indigenous communities.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"Dozens of executive orders liberalizing firearms rules increased the risk environment rather than reducing domestic fear.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"The sharp rise in gun ownership and leakage to criminal gangs shows downstream public-safety harm from that policy.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0039\",\"explanation\":\"After defeat, Bolsonaro urged supporters to unblock roads, which is a restraint signal rather than incitement to violence.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"The election-result challenge was weak on the merits and described as stoking protest rather than directing violence.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0002\",\"explanation\":\"The constitutional frame gave formal power, but it does not prove how far Bolsonaro could or did control state-level violence in practice.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Brazil's federal structure and decentralized policing limited direct national control over many abuses; the score reflects Bolsonaro's policy climate and tolerance, not every local act of violence.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Strong for discourse, agenda-setting, and federal policy; weaker for direct command over many state-level abuses.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.3\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.7\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.4\",\"3B.6\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0039\",\"explanation\":\"He explicitly told supporters to unblock roads after the election.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"The post-election challenge was more an agitation signal than a direct violence order.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0002\",\"explanation\":\"Formal constitutional authority existed, but formal power alone does not show direct violence control.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Constitutional text, rights NGOs, Reuters-style reporting, and court reporting; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the judgment rests entirely on the embedded evidence and contrary notes.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Bolsonaro lands in the lower-middle band because the dossier shows a hostile domestic-safety climate without evidence of full-scale state terror. The strongest negative facts are his repeated attacks on institutions and journalists, the weakening of Indigenous and environmental protections, and the gun-liberalization agenda that raised downstream violence risk. The counterevidence is real but limited: he told supporters to clear road blockades and some of the post-election messaging was restraining rather than violent. That keeps the score above the severe-authoritarian floor, but far below the high-restraint band, because the record still shows recurring intimidation, weakened protections, and state-environment harms rather than durable safety gains.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 2-3 because the record is coercive and harmful, but it does not show the kind of pervasive torture/disappearance regime required for the lower authoritarian bands.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 6+ because the evidence is dominated by intimidation, institutional undermining, and public-safety deterioration, with only modest restraint signals.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"recurring\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"state-aligned and tolerated\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"weak; courts and institutions resisted some abuse, but the executive climate remained hostile\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"strained; rhetoric, institutional attacks, and journalistic intimidation were central\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"worsened protection for vulnerable groups and the press, despite some post-defeat restraint\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"high for policy and rhetoric, moderate for downstream state violence\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.1 and 3B.2 are weaker than the incitement, vulnerable-group, and emergency-policy lenses\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:CHN:15832\",\"iso3\":\"CHN\",\"ruler_id\":\"1432\",\"ruler_year_id\":15832,\"ruler_name\":\"Xi Jinping\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IRN:19648\"],\"score_1_to_10\":2.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":87,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":1.5,\"upper\":2.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0027\",\"explanation\":\"The OHCHR Xinjiang assessment anchors the severity of the repression architecture that remained operative in 2022.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0032\",\"explanation\":\"Amnesty reported continuing systematic repression in Xinjiang with detention, torture, sexual violence, forced labor, and disappearances.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"HRW documented the coercive zero-Covid policy, including prolonged confinement, impeded care, and deaths after denial of treatment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"HRW also described Hong Kong repression and mass Xinjiang detention, reinforcing the system-wide pattern.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0039\",\"explanation\":\"Police inquiries and phone checks after the protests show surveillance-heavy coercive response to dissent.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0035\",\"explanation\":\"There was a limited women's-rights law and anti-trafficking campaign, but it did not offset the broader repression picture.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0041\",\"explanation\":\"The Urumqi easing was a partial local de-escalation, not a genuine national restraint reform.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"The dossier reflects a closed party-state with deep structural repression already in place; the score assesses Xi's 2022 maintenance and tightening of that system, not a single isolated incident.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Strong system-level attribution to Xi-era party-state governance, even when implementation occurred through local and provincial security organs.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.4\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.6\",\"3B.7\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.3\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0035\",\"explanation\":\"The women's-rights law and trafficking campaign show a small remedial step.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0041\",\"explanation\":\"Local easing after the Urumqi unrest is a restraint signal, but only partial and late.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"UN/IGO assessment, NGO annual reports, and Reuters/SCMP-style crisis reporting; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the judgment relies on the embedded evidence, attribution notes, and contrary evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"This is a very low score because the 2022 record is one of broad, system-level coercion. The dossier shows continuing Xinjiang repression, Hong Kong crackdown, zero-Covid confinement that harmed access to food and medical care, and protest surveillance after the November unrest. The few favorable items are narrow and mostly symbolic: a women's-rights law, an anti-trafficking campaign, and a local easing after unrest. Those do not change the chapter-level picture. The evidence supports a score above the absolute floor but squarely in the severe repression band because the state continued to use detention, surveillance, and coercive containment on a wide scale.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 1 because the dossier shows severe repression and mass harm, but not the historical mass-terror floor required for the lowest absolute anchor.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 3+ because the 2022 record is not merely selective abuse; it is a broad system of coercion across Xinjiang, Hong Kong, and protest response.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"systematic\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"direct/state-aligned\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"very weak; limited remedial steps were outweighed by sustained coercion\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"heavily censored and surveilled\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"continued repression with only narrow, late and partial easing\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"high system-level attribution through party-state control\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.3 personal incitement is less direct than the repression and detention lenses\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:COD:16113\",\"iso3\":\"COD\",\"ruler_id\":\"2830\",\"ruler_year_id\":16113,\"ruler_name\":\"Félix Tshisekedi\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\"],\"score_1_to_10\":4.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":80,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":3.5,\"upper\":5.0},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0032\",\"explanation\":\"State-agent abuses and extrajudicial executions were documented, showing that national forces were part of the harm environment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0033\",\"explanation\":\"The state of siege failed to improve security and enabled military authorities to curb expression and assembly.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0034\",\"explanation\":\"Police killings of protesters and weak accountability showed recurring abuse with a ruler-level response problem.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0047\",\"explanation\":\"The appointment of a police commander implicated in killings weakened the accountability signal further.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0035\",\"explanation\":\"The government did take some steps to investigate and punish abuses, even if impunity remained common.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0044\",\"explanation\":\"A draft press-law reform was adopted in cabinet, showing some protective institutional movement.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0045\",\"explanation\":\"Tshisekedi's crisis management on Kwamouth showed direct attention and humanitarian instruction rather than indifference.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"He ordered mourning and investigations after Kishishe, which is a real remedial response.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"The DRC's conflict environment and provincial security fragmentation are major constraints; the score reflects Tshisekedi's responsibility for state restraint, accountability, and the state of siege rather than every rebel or local crime.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mixed: some harms are clearly state-agent or military-environment harms, but many abuses are implemented by provincial actors and armed groups, not by Tshisekedi personally.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.4\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.3\",\"3B.6\",\"3B.7\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0035\",\"explanation\":\"Some investigative and punishment steps occurred.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0044\",\"explanation\":\"The cabinet-approved press-law draft is a concrete reform signal.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"Post-Kishishe investigation orders show corrective action, though after harm occurred.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"UN bulletins, presidential records, NGO reporting, and U.S. government country reporting; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the judgment is based on the cited evidence, attribution notes, and remedial counterevidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"This is a mixed but still clearly below-midpoint domestic-safety score. The adverse side is substantial: state-agent abuse, protest killings, the failure of the state of siege to improve security, weak accountability, and appointments that undercut the remedy signal. The favorable side is also real: Tshisekedi did order investigations, mourning, humanitarian response, and a press-law reform draft, and the government sometimes took steps against abusive officials. That combination keeps the score above the severe-repression band, but not near the protective high band, because the core 2022 problem is recurring abuse plus incomplete accountability rather than durable safety improvement.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 2-3 because the dossier includes real remedial action and some institutional reform, so it is not simply a one-way repression story.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 5+ because the security environment and impunity remain structurally poor, and the reforms do not show durable improvement in domestic safety.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"recurring\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"state-aligned and failed-to-prevent\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"partial and inconsistent; investigations and reforms existed but impunity remained common\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"constrained in conflict zones and under the state of siege\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"some remedial movement, but the year-end picture remains materially unsafe\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"moderate to high formal authority, but many abuses are provincial or military-chain dependent\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.3 and 3B.6 are weaker than the protest, siege, and accountability lenses\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"iso3\":\"DEU\",\"ruler_id\":\"2975\",\"ruler_year_id\":16984,\"ruler_name\":\"Olaf Scholz\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:JPN:16638\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:USA:15810\"],\"score_1_to_10\":8.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":85,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":7.5,\"upper\":8.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"The cabinet adopted a democratic-participation and extremism-prevention draft law, showing active institutional protection.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0033\",\"explanation\":\"Ukrainian-refugee access to benefits, healthcare, integration, and job support is a concrete protection measure.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0106\",\"explanation\":\"The whistleblower bill created internal and external reporting channels and anti-retaliation protections.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"The Federal Administrative Court protected protest camps as assemblies, showing strong rule-of-law restraint.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"A second federal package followed through on assistance and implementation planning for vulnerable people and victims.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0026\",\"explanation\":\"Amnesty still found serious police-accountability and discrimination gaps, so the system was not perfect.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0028\",\"explanation\":\"A fatal police operation showed that serious isolated abuse still occurred.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"The ECHR finding on ineffective investigation into racial profiling shows a real accountability gap.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0107\",\"explanation\":\"Police-capacity debates and calls for an independent commissioner show incomplete oversight reform.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Germany's inherited constitutional baseline was already strong; the score reflects Scholz's maintenance of a broadly protective order, plus some new remedial action, rather than a dramatic transformation.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mostly cabinet-level and institutional rather than personal; Scholz's responsibility is for the government environment and reform choices, not every local police action.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.4\",\"3B.6\",\"3B.7\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.3\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.9\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0026\",\"explanation\":\"Police-accountability gaps remain and lower the score from the top tier.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0028\",\"explanation\":\"A deadly police incident prevents an exceptional score.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"The ECHR failure on independent investigation is a meaningful but narrow weakness.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Official cabinet, court, and parliamentary records plus Amnesty and ECHR material; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the score is built from the cited evidence and the embedded contrary evidence only.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Germany is a high-score case because the 2022 record shows strong protection, active remedy, and robust institutional restraint. Scholz's government supported refugee access, whistleblower protection, democratic participation, and protest rights, while the courts continued to protect assembly and check police practice. The negative evidence is important but limited: police accountability and discriminatory-stop problems remained, and one serious fatal incident occurred. That is enough to stop a perfect score, but not enough to pull the case out of the high-restraint band. The overall record is of a broadly protective democracy with some real but bounded enforcement and oversight failures.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 6-7 because the dossier contains not just a good baseline, but several concrete new protective measures and meaningful remedy under pressure.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 9-10 because there were still serious police-accountability and discrimination gaps, so the year was not a near-flawless safety record.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"isolated to recurring low-level abuse, not systemic terror\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"failed-to-prevent and partially remedied\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"stronger than average; courts and government actions provided real though incomplete remedy\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"open and highly visible\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"mostly stable and slightly improved through new protection measures\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"moderate; much of the conduct is institutional rather than chancellor-personal\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.1 and 3B.2 are not the dominant story; the main evidence is oversight, remedy, and refugee protection\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:EGY:16232\",\"iso3\":\"EGY\",\"ruler_id\":\"2851\",\"ruler_year_id\":16232,\"ruler_name\":\"Abdel Fattah el-Sisi\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IRN:19648\"],\"score_1_to_10\":2.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":86,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":2.0,\"upper\":3.0},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0026\",\"explanation\":\"The leaked-prison-video case shows state abuse and a bad investigative response rather than protection.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0028\",\"explanation\":\"Mass detentions ahead of COP27 show recurring repression of peaceful protest.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"The pre-COP27 arrests underscore broad repression and the scale of the security crackdown.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"The official country report lists unlawful killings, disappearances, torture, arbitrary detention, political prisoners, and weak punishment of abusive officials.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"Human Rights Watch described ongoing disappearances, torture, prison abuse, and repression around COP27.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"The pardon committee released 30 pretrial detainees, showing modest remedial action.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"The president's 'year of civil society' declaration is a restraint signal, albeit rhetorical.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"Prison reforms, a rights strategy, and the end of the state of emergency are meaningful but limited counterweights.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Egypt's security state and detention system were already coercive; the score reflects Sisi's maintenance of that environment in 2022, not a new collapse from a previously open baseline.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Strong at the state level and through the security apparatus; the record is about Sisi's government and institutions, not a single personal order in each case.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.4\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.3\",\"3B.6\",\"3B.7\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"A small detainee-release action shows some remedial capacity.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"The civil-society declaration is a weak positive signal.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"Prison and rights reforms were announced, though critics viewed them as hollow.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Official state biographies and statements, U.S. government reporting, HRW, Amnesty, and Reuters-style coverage; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the score relies entirely on the cited evidence and the embedded contrary notes.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Egypt in 2022 remains a severe repression case. The dossier shows custodial abuse, mass arrests around COP27, continued disappearances and torture, harsh prison conditions, and persistent failure to punish abusive officials. The government did make some limited gestures: a small detainee release, a civil-society proclamation, prison-reform rhetoric, and the lifting of the emergency law. Those gestures matter, but they do not change the core chapter finding because they were narrow, late, and not matched by a serious accountability shift. The evidence therefore fits the severe-authoritarian band, below midpoint but above the absolute floor.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 1-2 because the record is very coercive, but it does not meet the chapter's mass-terror floor.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 4+ because the remedial steps are too limited and the abuse pattern too persistent to place Egypt in a materially safer band.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"systematic repression\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"direct/state-aligned\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"weak and largely cosmetic\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"heavily constrained; protest and civil-society space remained narrow\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"small reform gestures without durable safety gains\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"high state-level attribution; personal-order evidence is less direct than system-level control\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.3 personal incitement is not the main driver; the story is detention, torture, and repression\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:ETH:16782\",\"iso3\":\"ETH\",\"ruler_id\":\"2310\",\"ruler_year_id\":16782,\"ruler_name\":\"Abiy Ahmed\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\"],\"score_1_to_10\":3.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":74,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":2.5,\"upper\":4.0},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0034\",\"explanation\":\"Western Tigray abuses with acquiescence and possible participation of federal forces are among the strongest adverse facts.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0035\",\"explanation\":\"Tigrayan deportees were arbitrarily detained and abused, showing persistent ethnic profiling and security failure.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"The hostile environment for journalists and civil society, plus weak accountability, shows broader repression.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"The federal government restricted aid to Tigray and held thousands arbitrarily detained, while accountability remained weak.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0032\",\"explanation\":\"The large-scale crackdown in Amhara and journalist arrests show recurring coercive state behavior.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0008\",\"explanation\":\"The humanitarian truce is a real de-escalatory step and lowers the score from a more severe band.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0027\",\"explanation\":\"Some prisoner releases and a dialogue-oriented framing show restraint and political opening at least in part.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"UNHCR cooperation on refugee protection is a narrow positive protection signal.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"The year sits inside a war-and-postwar security crisis with fragmented federal, regional, and armed-group responsibility; the score reflects Abiy's responsibility for restraint, detention policy, and accountability rather than every local violent episode.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mixed and partly indirect: some harms are federal and some are regional or armed-group driven, but Abiy still governs the system and its response choices.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.4\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.3\",\"3B.6\",\"3B.7\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0008\",\"explanation\":\"The humanitarian truce matters as restraint, even though it was not durable by itself.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0027\",\"explanation\":\"Prisoner releases are a real restraint signal.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"UNHCR cooperation on refugees shows some protective action.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Official statements, UN/IGO reporting, and rights NGOs; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the judgment is based on the cited evidence and the embedded contrary notes.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Abiy Ahmed's 2022 score is low because the year contains real state-related abuse, ethnic profiling, arbitrary detention, aid restrictions, and a weak accountability record. The affirmative evidence is not absent, but it is limited: a humanitarian truce, some prisoner releases, and a few protection-oriented actions. Those steps keep the score from falling into the deepest repression band, yet they do not overcome the large body of evidence on detention, censorship, and the federal government's failure to stop abuses. The right way to read the record is as a conflict-state with some restraint moves, not as a safely protective domestic order.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 1-2 because the dossier shows severe coercion, but not the mass-terror floor for the bottom anchor.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 4+ because the truce and releases are real but too partial to offset the broad coercive record and weak remedy.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"recurring with localized systematic elements\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"state-aligned and failed-to-prevent\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"weak; some releases and truce steps, but limited durable remedy\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"constrained, especially around conflict and dissent\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"some de-escalation, but not enough to convert the year into a protective one\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"moderate to high system-level attribution, but with significant regional and armed-group complicating factors\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.3 and 3B.7 are weaker than the detention, aid, and accountability lenses\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IDN:16199\",\"iso3\":\"IDN\",\"ruler_id\":\"2636\",\"ruler_year_id\":16199,\"ruler_name\":\"Joko Widodo\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\"],\"score_1_to_10\":4.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":79,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":4.0,\"upper\":5.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0028\",\"explanation\":\"Wadas showed intimidation, arbitrary detention, and beatings in a state project setting.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"Komnas HAM found police violence in Wadas and documented trauma among residents.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0032\",\"explanation\":\"Kanjuruhan was a mass-casualty domestic safety failure triggered by police crowd control.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"Papuan abuses, including arrests, torture, extrajudicial killings, and displacement, remained a serious issue.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"Soldiers were arrested over the killing and mutilation of Papuans, indicating both harm and some accountability.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0034\",\"explanation\":\"Jokowi ordered a thorough investigation of the Brigadir J police-killing scandal and said nothing should be covered up.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0035\",\"explanation\":\"The accountability critique around Brigadir J shows the problem was recognized, even if not solved.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0039\",\"explanation\":\"His democracy-focused statement on protests is a mild restraint signal.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Indonesia's large, decentralized security environment means some abuses are provincial or military rather than personally directed; the score reflects Jokowi's national responsibility for restraint, remedy, and policing standards.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mixed: direct for some appointments and public instructions, indirect for much of the coercive policing and military conduct.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.4\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.3\",\"3B.6\",\"3B.7\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0034\",\"explanation\":\"The president ordered investigators to get to the truth in Brigadir J.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0035\",\"explanation\":\"There was at least some public recognition of police-accountability failure.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0039\",\"explanation\":\"Democracy-and-protest rhetoric is a weak but real restraint signal.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Official appointments, rights NGOs, national commission findings, and Reuters-style event reporting; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the score is anchored in the cited evidence and the contrary notes embedded in the dossier.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Indonesia is a mixed case with significant recurring domestic-safety failures but not a fully repressive order. The evidence includes police violence around Wadas, the Kanjuruhan stadium catastrophe, serious Papuan abuses, and only partial accountability afterward. There are also important restraint and correction signals: Jokowi ordered a thorough Brigadir J investigation, publicly emphasized democracy and protest, and some soldiers were arrested in a Papua case. Those counterweights matter, but they do not erase the repeated serious harms. The right band is the middle band, below a genuinely protective democracy but above a hard-repression case.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 3-4 because there is some real restraint and accountability, so the year is not a pure coercive decline.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 6+ because recurring serious abuse, especially police and military conduct, kept the environment materially unsafe.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"recurring\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"state-aligned and failed-to-prevent\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"partial; some investigations and arrests, but follow-through remains uneven\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"open enough for criticism, but protest and minority activism faced pressure\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"mixed, with some corrective action but persistent serious safety failures\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"moderate; direct for some executive choices, indirect for many provincial/security abuses\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.3 incitement is weaker than the policing, custody, and vulnerable-group lenses\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\",\"iso3\":\"IND\",\"ruler_id\":\"1517\",\"ruler_year_id\":19645,\"ruler_name\":\"Narendra Modi\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\"],\"score_1_to_10\":4.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":72,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":4.0,\"upper\":5.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"Jammu and Kashmir remained under a coercive security environment with poor accountability for some killings.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"Amnesty described shrinking space for dissent, minority discrimination, and harmful legal changes.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0032\",\"explanation\":\"USCIRF reported promotion of Hindu nationalism and laws that contributed to systematic religious-freedom violations.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"The government-linked reaction to communal outrage in Kanpur shows a volatile environment around religious incitement and order.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"The partial reduction of AFSPA coverage in some northeast districts is a real restraint signal.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0033\",\"explanation\":\"The Supreme Court checked use of sedition law and urged governments not to register new cases under it.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0035\",\"explanation\":\"Retrospective treaty-body criticism is serious, but it is less direct than the contemporaneous evidence.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"India's federal structure, state policing, and inherited communal tensions complicate attribution; the score reflects the Modi-era national environment and central choices, not every local abuse.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Moderate to strong for legal climate, rhetoric, and central policy; weaker for decentralized violence and local policing outcomes.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.4\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.7\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.3\",\"3B.6\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"AFSPA reduction is a concrete, though limited, restraint step.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0033\",\"explanation\":\"The sedition order shows judicial restraint rather than unbounded executive coercion.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0035\",\"explanation\":\"Retrospective criticism exists, but it is not a direct contemporaneous operational finding.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Human-rights NGOs, USCIRF, Supreme Court reporting, and Reuters-style current affairs; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the score is based on the cited dossier evidence and its embedded contrary notes.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"India in 2022 is a middle score because the dossier contains real coercive and discriminatory patterns but not systematic state terror. The adverse evidence is substantial: contested protest and speech restrictions, harm to Muslims and minorities, an aggressive legal climate, Kashmir repression, and weak accountability for communal and policing harms. At the same time, there are meaningful legal-restraint signals, especially the reduction of AFSPA coverage in some districts and the Supreme Court's sedition intervention. Those do not make the system protective, but they do keep it above the harsher authoritarian bands. The score therefore sits in the recurring-failure range: serious, persistent, but not totalized repression.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 3-4 because the country still has visible judicial checks and some restraint steps, so the evidence is not as uniformly coercive as a lower authoritarian band.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 6+ because protest suppression, minority targeting, and accountability failures are too persistent to treat the year as broadly protective.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"recurring\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"state-aligned and partly tolerated\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"mixed; some court restraint, but recurring remedy gaps and local impunity\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"contested and increasingly constrained for critics\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"partial restraint on some security laws, but broader safety climate remained problematic\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"moderate; central climate matters, but local and state-level actors drive much of the harm\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.1 and 3B.2 are less direct than the legal-climate and minority-protection evidence\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IRN:19648\",\"iso3\":\"IRN\",\"ruler_id\":\"3412\",\"ruler_year_id\":19648,\"ruler_name\":\"Ali Khamenei\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:CHN:15832\"],\"score_1_to_10\":2.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":88,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":1.5,\"upper\":2.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0025\",\"explanation\":\"Leaked documents reportedly showed top-level orders to severely confront protesters.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0026\",\"explanation\":\"Security forces used live ammunition and killed or injured hundreds of peaceful protesters.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"Zahedan saw a bloody crackdown with at least 66 deaths and many injuries, including children and bystanders.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0032\",\"explanation\":\"Amnesty reported sham death-penalty trials used to intimidate protesters.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0033\",\"explanation\":\"HRW reported executions after grossly unfair trials and more protest-related death sentences.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"The UN fact-finding-mission mandate itself records hundreds of deaths, arrests, and severe repression.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0022\",\"explanation\":\"Khamenei's own rhetoric backed the security forces and framed the protests as enemy-planned riots.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0034\",\"explanation\":\"He publicly praised the Basij for confronting 'rioters', reinforcing the crackdown climate.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0035\",\"explanation\":\"A later synthesis on 2022 shutdowns shows the breadth of internet suppression, but it does not meaningfully soften the repression record.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Iran is a closed, highly repressive system; the score reflects Khamenei's maintenance and endorsement of that system in 2022, not a single isolated incident.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Strong system-level attribution to Khamenei through security, judicial, and propaganda control, even where local actors executed specific abuses.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.4\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.6\",\"3B.7\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.3\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0022\",\"explanation\":\"His speech also included a personal note of grief over Amini's death, but that does not offset the backing of the crackdown.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0034\",\"explanation\":\"Basij praise is rhetoric, not a formal written order, but it still strengthens the repression signal.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0035\",\"explanation\":\"Internet shutdown evidence is broad but mostly contextual rather than a separate restraint or remedy signal.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"UN/IGO records, Amnesty, HRW, Access Now, and Reuters-style reporting; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the score is derived solely from the embedded evidence and contrary notes.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Khamenei's 2022 domestic-safety record is severe repression, with the state using lethal force, mass arrests, internet shutdowns, and death sentences to crush protest. The dossier includes both direct rhetoric from Khamenei backing the security forces and a body of corroborating UN and NGO evidence about the violence. There are no meaningful protective counterweights in the file, only rhetoric and some later procedural formality. The score is therefore low but not at the historical mass-terror floor: it reflects systematic repression with broad impunity, not the absolute bottom reserved for exterminatory or equivalent catastrophe-level rule.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 1 because the dossier shows grave, widespread repression, but not the chapter's historical mass-terror floor for the absolute lowest anchor.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 3+ because the record is not merely selective abuse; it is a coordinated, nationwide crackdown with lethal and judicial components.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"systematic repression\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"direct/state-aligned\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"minimal to none; repression dominates remedy\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"heavily censored, surveilled, and intermittently shut down\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"worsened sharply during the protest wave and remained coercive through year-end\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"very high system-level attribution through the Supreme Leader's control of coercive institutions\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.3 personal incitement is secondary to the broader state terror record\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:JPN:16638\",\"iso3\":\"JPN\",\"ruler_id\":\"2895\",\"ruler_year_id\":16638,\"ruler_name\":\"Fumio Kishida\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:USA:15810\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\"],\"score_1_to_10\":8.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":86,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":8.0,\"upper\":9.0},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"Kishida's immediate response to the Abe assassination was public, direct, and crisis-focused.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"He reshuffled the cabinet and ordered vetting on Unification Church links, showing active political-risk management.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"He apologized, ordered severance of church ties, and created victim-relief mechanisms.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0032\",\"explanation\":\"By year-end he had moved from rhetoric to a new law and a government-wide support structure for victims.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0033\",\"explanation\":\"Police review and strengthened protection rules after the assassination show real corrective action.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"Japan's refugee response to Ukrainians is concrete evidence of protective state capacity under his government.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0034\",\"explanation\":\"The U.S. human-rights report still noted a clean bill of health on killings and torture, but also did not hide some due-process concerns.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"Pre-indictment detention concerns and ethnic profiling complaints prevent a perfect score.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"The later policy white paper confirms follow-through, but the actions were still reactive rather than proof of a flawless prior system.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Japan's inherited baseline is highly protective and open; the score reflects Kishida's maintenance of that baseline plus meaningful corrective action after a major shock.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Strong for cabinet and policy response, though many protections are institutional rather than personal and some criminal-justice features remain inherited.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.4\",\"3B.6\",\"3B.7\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.3\",\"3B.5\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"There are still detention and profiling concerns, especially before indictment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0034\",\"explanation\":\"The overall baseline is already safe, so a perfect score would require more than a good status quo.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"The corrective package is strong but reactive, not proof of prior perfection.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Primary PM transcripts, police and cabinet materials, and official country reporting; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the judgment rests on the embedded evidence and the contrary notes in the dossier.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Japan is among the strongest cases in the batch because the 2022 record combines an already protective baseline with serious corrective action after the Abe assassination and the Unification Church scandal. Kishida's government did not just issue statements: it reshuffled the cabinet, ordered vetting, created victim-relief machinery, and helped drive a new law and police review. The main negatives are limited and important, especially detention and profiling concerns in criminal justice, which prevent a perfect score. But the country remains one where rare serious abuse sits inside a generally robust restraint-and-remedy framework, so the score belongs in the high band.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 7-8 because the dossier shows not just stability but active, consequential protective and remedial action under pressure.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 9-10 because some due-process and detention concerns remain, and some of the strongest actions were reactive rather than evidence of flawless prior protection.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"isolated and remedied, not systematic\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"failed-to-prevent some isolated harms, but generally protective and remedial\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"strong; direct corrective action followed the assassination shock and church scandal\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"open and visible\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"maintained high safety baseline with meaningful post-crisis correction\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"high for executive response, though much of the safety baseline is institutional\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.1-3B.3 are not central; the main story is remedy, protest tolerance, and institutional protection\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:MEX:15801\",\"iso3\":\"MEX\",\"ruler_id\":\"2779\",\"ruler_year_id\":15801,\"ruler_name\":\"Andrés Manuel López Obrador\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\"],\"score_1_to_10\":4.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":76,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":3.5,\"upper\":4.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0024\",\"explanation\":\"Press attacks were severe and state actors were responsible for a large share of them.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0025\",\"explanation\":\"Journalist killings and failed protection for media workers show a serious safety failure environment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0028\",\"explanation\":\"The National Guard militarization proposal would increase coercive capacity and blur civilian control.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"The Ayotzinapa obstruction and replacement choice undermine confidence in accountability.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"Illicit surveillance of journalists and defenders is a direct domestic-safety concern.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"The Ayotzinapa truth commission and document review were genuine transparency steps.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0034\",\"explanation\":\"The UN communication shows protection failures for women searchers, but it is allegation-focused rather than a final merits finding.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Mexico's severe crime and cartel environment complicate attribution; the score isolates AMLO-era federal choices, protection failures, and tolerated coercive trends rather than all local violence.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mixed: direct for federal policy proposals and transparency choices, indirect for many killings and intimidation events driven by local or criminal actors.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.4\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.3\",\"3B.6\",\"3B.7\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"The Ayotzinapa truth effort is a real accountability step.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0034\",\"explanation\":\"The UN communication is serious but does not alone prove a personal presidential order.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Rights NGOs, UN communications, official presidential material, and Reuters-style reporting; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the score relies on the cited evidence and the dossier's embedded contrary notes.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Mexico sits in the middle band because the dossier shows substantial domestic-safety failures, but not a single centralized terror system. The worst facts are repeated journalist killings, state-actor press abuse, surveillance, militarization, and serious obstruction around Ayotzinapa. The positive side is thin but real: the administration did pursue a truth commission and public transparency steps, which stops the score from falling further. Yet the year still reads as a dangerous mix of coercive state behavior, criminal violence, and weak remedy, so the chapter score remains clearly below a protective democracy but above the severe-authoritarian floor.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 2-3 because the file contains some real transparency and legal action, so it is not a purely collapsed remedy environment.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 5+ because repeated journalist killings, surveillance, and militarization are too serious to call the year broadly safe.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"recurring\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"mixed: state-aligned plus non-state violence\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"partial and uneven; transparency exists, but protection failures dominate\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"open but hostile for journalists and defenders\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"mixed; some truth-seeking, but coercive and surveilling tendencies persist\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"moderate; federal choices matter, but criminal and local actors drive much of the harm\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.3 personal incitement is weaker than the policy, surveillance, and accountability-lens evidence\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:NGA:15787\",\"iso3\":\"NGA\",\"ruler_id\":\"2776\",\"ruler_year_id\":15787,\"ruler_name\":\"Muhammadu Buhari\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\"],\"score_1_to_10\":3.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":81,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":3.0,\"upper\":4.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0019\",\"explanation\":\"The official rights report documents killings, disappearances, torture, arbitrary arrest, and weak accountability under Buhari's administration.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0020\",\"explanation\":\"Security-service torture and arbitrary arrest, including Amnesty-documented southeastern abuse, remained serious in 2022.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0021\",\"explanation\":\"Excessive force during protests and no investigation into a shooting show recurring policing failures.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0022\",\"explanation\":\"Journalist detention, NBC fines, and media intimidation show a constrained information environment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0024\",\"explanation\":\"The Lekki accountability failure and partial compensation show weak remedy.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0027\",\"explanation\":\"Buhari condemned mob violence and called for an impartial probe, which is a restraint signal.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0028\",\"explanation\":\"He explicitly rejected lethal force in the Kaduna hostage crisis, prioritizing the safety of abductees.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"His Independence Day address urged peaceful grievance handling and anti-incitement behavior.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"He instructed the armed forces to remain apolitical and uphold human-rights obligations.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0033\",\"explanation\":\"A court challenge over the Twitter agreement and the eventual lifting of the ban show some legal and political limits.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Nigeria's security environment was already highly violent and decentralized; the score reflects Buhari's responsibility for restraint, remedy, and tolerated impunity rather than all insurgent or communal violence.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mixed: direct for speeches and some security instructions, indirect for many abusive acts by security services and local actors.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.4\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.3\",\"3B.6\",\"3B.7\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0027\",\"explanation\":\"He condemned a mob killing and called for investigation.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0028\",\"explanation\":\"He explicitly favored restraint in the hostage crisis.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"He gave a direct apolitical and human-rights instruction to the armed forces.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Official state statements, U.S. government reporting, Amnesty, HRW, and Reuters-style current affairs; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the score is grounded in the cited evidence and the contrary notes embedded in the dossier.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Nigeria under Buhari is a recurring-failure case. The record contains serious killings, disappearances, torture, media intimidation, protest repression, and weak accountability, but it is not a clean terror state. Buhari also issued several restraint signals: he condemned mob violence, explicitly rejected lethal force in a hostage crisis, ordered the armed forces to stay apolitical, and urged peaceful grievance handling. Those positives keep the score above the harsh authoritarian floor, but the continued pattern of abuse and impunity keeps it well below a genuinely protective order. The chapter answer is therefore a middling-low score rather than a collapse or a success story.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 2-3 because there are repeated restraint statements and some limited legal pressure, so the case is not a pure coercive breakdown.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 5+ because the evidence still shows recurring torture, killings, protest suppression, and weak remedy.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"recurring and partly systemic in security practice\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"state-aligned and tolerated\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"weak; remedies are partial and often come after public pressure\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"strained; journalists and critics faced harassment and censorship pressure\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"some restraint rhetoric, but no durable safety improvement\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"moderate to high for the federal security environment, but many abuses are operationally decentralized\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.3 incitement is not the main story; the chapter turns on detention, protest, and remedy failures\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:PAK:16682\",\"iso3\":\"PAK\",\"ruler_id\":\"2911\",\"ruler_year_id\":16682,\"ruler_name\":\"Shehbaz Sharif\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\"],\"score_1_to_10\":4.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":78,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":3.5,\"upper\":5.0},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0026\",\"explanation\":\"The official country report records serious killings, disappearances, torture, arbitrary detention, and political prisoners under the 2022 government environment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"Specific torture allegations after high-profile arrests show coercive policing and detention practice.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"The passage of the torture and custodial-death bill is a concrete remedial step.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0101\",\"explanation\":\"The enforced-disappearance committee shows the federal government responded after court notice, albeit indirectly.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0105\",\"explanation\":\"The Adiala inquiry findings and referral path show genuine accountability pressure, even if incomplete.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0034\",\"explanation\":\"Media pressure and blocking continued through 2022, which weakens any claim of a safe civic space.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0103\",\"explanation\":\"Families of the disappeared and activists faced state violence and intimidation.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0104\",\"explanation\":\"The IHC torture inquiry shows that coercive custody remained an active problem requiring judicial intervention.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0106\",\"explanation\":\"The presidential warning letter on journalists underscores the seriousness of the safety deficit.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Pakistan's security institutions were already strong, opaque, and partly autonomous; the score reflects Sharif's responsibility for restraint, remedy, and tolerated impunity rather than every preexisting abuse.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mixed: direct for some legislative and committee choices, indirect for many security-service abuses and custody conditions.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.4\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.3\",\"3B.6\",\"3B.7\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"The torture-and-custodial-death bill is a real reform step.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0101\",\"explanation\":\"The disappearance committee shows institutional response after judicial pressure.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0105\",\"explanation\":\"The Adiala inquiry and referral chain show active remedy pressure, though not full success.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Official country reporting, courts and commissions, and Amnesty/HRW reporting; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the judgment relies on the cited evidence and the embedded contrary notes.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Pakistan under Shehbaz Sharif is a serious but not fully collapsed domestic-safety case. The dossier shows torture allegations, enforced disappearances, media intimidation, and a continued pattern of weak accountability. At the same time, Sharif's government did enact a torture-and-custodial-death bill and moved, under judicial pressure, toward committees and inquiries on disappearances and custody abuse. Those remedies are real and matter, but they are not yet enough to transform the safety environment. The score therefore lands in the recurring-coercion band, below a protective democracy and above the harshest authoritarian cases.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 2-3 because the file contains meaningful legal and inquiry responses, so it is not a pure repression record.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 5+ because the evidence still shows serious custody abuse, media pressure, and unresolved disappearances.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"recurring\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"state-aligned and tolerated\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"partial; reforms exist, but enforcement and independence remain thin\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"strained for journalists and dissidents\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"some remedial movement under court pressure, but not enough for durable safety improvement\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"moderate; some direct governmental action, but many abuses are institutional and security-service driven\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.3 personal incitement is not central; the main issue is custody, remedy, and protest repression\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:PHL:16194\",\"iso3\":\"PHL\",\"ruler_id\":\"2844\",\"ruler_year_id\":16194,\"ruler_name\":\"Bongbong Marcos\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\"],\"score_1_to_10\":4.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":74,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":4.0,\"upper\":5.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0025\",\"explanation\":\"The incoming security-adviser shift and anti-red-tagging comments show at least some early restraint intent.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0026\",\"explanation\":\"The CHR warning on red-tagging shows the issue remained a genuine safety concern.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0027\",\"explanation\":\"Permitted protest handling ahead of the SONA was relatively restrained, though still tightly policed.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"Police blocking a march during the first 100 days shows continued pressure on protest space.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0033\",\"explanation\":\"The continued high number of drug-related killings means the safety environment remained serious.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0024\",\"explanation\":\"Marcos's human-rights promise is a real positive signal, but it is only a promise.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"The ICC non-rejoin decision indicates a limited appetite for external accountability on inherited abuses.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"The Philippines inherited a very violent drug-war environment and a polarized rights climate; the score reflects Marcos's responsibility for restraint and remedy in 2022, not all inherited killings or local policing.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mixed: some direct appointments and statements, but much of the observed harm is through police and inherited security practice rather than a personally documented order from Marcos.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.2\",\"3B.4\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.3\",\"3B.6\",\"3B.7\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0024\",\"explanation\":\"He publicly vowed to protect human rights on entering office.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0025\",\"explanation\":\"His team expressed interest in ending red-tagging, which is a modest restraint sign.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0027\",\"explanation\":\"Some protest handling was permitted rather than purely repressive.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Philippine state news, CHR material, human-rights NGOs, Reuters, and a university-based monitoring project; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the judgment is based entirely on the cited evidence and the embedded contrary notes.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Marcos lands in the middle because the dossier shows a mixed first year with some restraint language and some genuine domestic-safety concerns that persisted. The negative side includes continued drug-related killings, protest obstruction, red-tagging concerns, and a decision not to rejoin the ICC that signals caution about accountability for inherited abuses. The positive side is not trivial: he appointed a security adviser with anti-red-tagging views, vowed to protect human rights, and some protest events were allowed. Still, the year does not show a durable improvement in safety, so the score sits below a protective democracy but above the harsher repression cases.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 3-4 because the record contains some real restraint and institutional openness, so it is not as coercive as a darker authoritarian band.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 6+ because the continuation of drug killings and protest pressure is too serious to call the year broadly safe.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"recurring\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"state-aligned and tolerated\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"mixed; some restraint signals, but accountability for inherited abuses remains weak\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"open but pressured\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"some early moderation signals, but no durable safety turnaround in 2022\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"moderate; direct for appointments and statements, indirect for much of the harm\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.1 and 3B.3 are weaker than the protest, drug-violence, and accountability lenses\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"iso3\":\"RUS\",\"ruler_id\":\"2759\",\"ruler_year_id\":15706,\"ruler_name\":\"Vladimir Putin\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IRN:19648\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:CHN:15832\"],\"score_1_to_10\":2.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":90,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":1.5,\"upper\":2.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"Post-invasion censorship and anti-war protest suppression are clear signs of a coercive state response.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0032\",\"explanation\":\"Mass arbitrary arrests and media blocking show broad repression rather than narrow law enforcement.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0034\",\"explanation\":\"The Justice Ministry shutdown of foreign NGO offices narrowed independent oversight and complaint channels.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"Ending ECHR jurisdiction removed a major remedy pathway for victims.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"Mobilization protests were dispersed with large-scale detentions.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"The annual repression summary documents huge arrest and blocking totals across 2022.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0041\",\"explanation\":\"The expanded anti-LGBT propaganda law directly intensified state targeting of a vulnerable group.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"The country-report summary confirms the same repression pattern at the system level, leaving little room for a higher score.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Russia in 2022 was already a closed repressive system, and the invasion intensified domestic coercion; the score reflects Putin's responsibility for the resulting climate of fear and suppression.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Very high system-level attribution through executive, legislative, police, and regulatory control, even where implementation was carried out by subordinate organs.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.3\",\"3B.4\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.7\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.6\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0041\",\"explanation\":\"The anti-LGBT law is a direct signature act and makes the repression even clearer rather than softer.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"Removing ECHR jurisdiction is a remedy rollback, not a restraint signal.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"The annual repression count is broad and severe, leaving no credible protective counterweight.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Amnesty, HRW, Reuters-style reporting, OVD-Info, and official U.S. government country reporting; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the score is grounded in the cited evidence and the contrary notes embedded in the dossier.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Putin's 2022 domestic-safety record is one of broad, organized repression. The evidence shows censorship, mass arrests, protest suppression, shutdowns of oversight channels, removal of ECHR remedies, and direct legal targeting of a vulnerable minority group. There is no meaningful corrective counterweight in the file, only more coercive acts. This is not the absolute historical floor because the dossier does not show exterminatory mass violence at the required level, but it is very clearly in the severe-authoritarian band. The score is low because the state used the year to tighten fear and suppress dissent rather than protect citizens from domestic violence or abuse.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 1 because the file shows grave, system-wide repression, but not the historical mass-terror floor required for the lowest anchor.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 3+ because the repression is broad and organized, not merely a set of isolated abuses or a mixed record with substantial restraint.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"systematic repression\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"direct/state-aligned\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"very weak; remedy channels were actively narrowed\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"severely constrained and censored\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"worsened sharply after the invasion and remained coercive through year-end\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"very high; the record is strongly attributable to the Putin system and its coercive organs\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.6 is less central than the protest, censorship, and remedy-rollback lenses\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:THA:19649\",\"iso3\":\"THA\",\"ruler_id\":\"3414\",\"ruler_year_id\":19649,\"ruler_name\":\"Prayut Chan-o-cha\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\"],\"score_1_to_10\":3.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":79,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":3.0,\"upper\":4.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0025\",\"explanation\":\"Large-scale prosecutions of protest and dissent cases show a restrictive political-security climate.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0026\",\"explanation\":\"Torture and disappearances remained unresolved, with little punishment, which is a major domestic-safety failure.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0027\",\"explanation\":\"The southern border conflict was partially de-escalated, but accountability for abuses remained weak.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0028\",\"explanation\":\"Asylum seekers, including Rohingya and Uighurs, faced arrest and indefinite immigration detention.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0033\",\"explanation\":\"The emergency decree and martial law in the south gave security forces broad powers and near-immunity.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0032\",\"explanation\":\"Lifting the nationwide COVID emergency decree is a real restraint signal.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"The torture and custodial-death bill shows some reform capacity.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"The government-report summary still describes a constrained protest environment, limiting any high score.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Thailand inherited a strong security-state framework and longstanding southern emergency regime; the score reflects Prayut's maintenance of that coercive structure in 2022.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Direct at the national-government level for protest, emergency, and detention policy; indirect for many local abuses and southern conflict incidents.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.4\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.7\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.3\",\"3B.6\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0032\",\"explanation\":\"The emergency-decree repeal is a genuine restraint step.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"The torture/disappearance bill is meaningful, even if enforcement is still thin.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0027\",\"explanation\":\"Partial de-escalation in the south prevents a more extreme score.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Human-rights NGOs, official U.S. government reporting, and Thai/local reporting; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the score is derived from the cited evidence and the embedded contrary notes.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Thailand under Prayut is a coercive but not mass-terror case. The dossier shows large protest prosecutions, unresolved torture and disappearance problems, harsh treatment of asylum seekers, and an emergency regime in the south with broad powers. There are genuine counterweights: a nationwide emergency-decree repeal, a torture/disappearance bill, and some local de-escalation in the south. Those steps matter, but they do not erase the core picture of a restrictive security state with persistent impunity. The score therefore sits in the recurring-repression band rather than in the high-restraint band.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 2-3 because the dossier contains some real legal and emergency-power restraint, so it is not a pure breakdown case.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 5+ because protest prosecutions, unresolved torture, and detention-heavy immigration policy remain too serious for a protective score.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"recurring\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"direct/state-aligned\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"weak to partial; some reform exists but impunity remains a dominant pattern\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"constrained for protest and dissent\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"some reform and partial de-escalation, but not enough to shift the chapter materially upward\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"high at the national level, though some harms are regionally implemented\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.3 is not the main driver; the key story is detention, protest, and emergency-power abuse\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:TUR:15972\",\"iso3\":\"TUR\",\"ruler_id\":\"2813\",\"ruler_year_id\":15972,\"ruler_name\":\"Recep Tayyip Erdoğan\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\"],\"score_1_to_10\":3.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":78,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":3.0,\"upper\":4.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0023\",\"explanation\":\"The broader authoritarian media and judiciary climate under Erdoğan is a core adverse fact.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0024\",\"explanation\":\"State torture and arbitrary detention were reported by official and independent sources.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0025\",\"explanation\":\"Police repression of the women's march and Saturday Mothers is concrete protest suppression.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0027\",\"explanation\":\"The disinformation law materially increased censorship risk and chilled expression.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"The Gülşen detention shows politically charged judicial coercion against a critic.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"Erdoğan's public rhetoric after the Istanbul bombing was hard-edged and security-centric.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0032\",\"explanation\":\"The Istanbul response is a real domestic-crisis reaction rather than pure incitement.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"The anti-LGBT constitutional rhetoric is harmful, but it is rhetoric plus legal signaling, not a direct violence order.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Turkey already had a strongly centralized, security-heavy political environment; the score reflects Erdoğan's maintenance of that environment in 2022 rather than every local act of policing.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Direct for legal climate and rhetoric, indirect for many detention and protest-control events executed by police and courts.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.2\",\"3B.4\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.3\",\"3B.6\",\"3B.7\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0032\",\"explanation\":\"The bombing response is a genuine security reaction, not just anti-opposition signaling.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"The anti-LGBT remarks are coercive and stigmatizing, but they are rhetoric rather than a direct violence order.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Official country reporting, Amnesty, Council of Europe, CPJ, Reuters-style reporting, and Irish/other republishers; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the score is based on the cited evidence and the embedded contrary notes.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Turkey in 2022 is a recurring-repression case with meaningful but limited checks. The dossier shows authoritarian media and judiciary control, protest suppression, torture and arbitrary detention, a disinformation law that chilled expression, and politically charged arrests. There are a few crisis-response and legal-signaling counterpoints, but they do not meaningfully change the domestic-safety picture. The evidence therefore supports a score in the lower-middle band: seriously coercive, but not a mass-terror case and not a total collapse of state protection.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 2-3 because the file contains some institutional activity and crisis response, so it is not pure terror or exterminatory rule.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 5+ because media control, protest suppression, and detention abuse are too consistent for a protective score.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"recurring\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"direct/state-aligned\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"weak; some legal process exists, but it often serves repression\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"constrained and increasingly chilled\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"continued coercion with occasional crisis-managed restraint\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"high for the legal and rhetorical environment, moderate for direct physical abuses\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.1 and 3B.6 are weaker than the protest, media, and detention lenses\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:USA:15810\",\"iso3\":\"USA\",\"ruler_id\":\"2782\",\"ruler_year_id\":15810,\"ruler_name\":\"Joe Biden\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:JPN:16638\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\"],\"score_1_to_10\":7.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":82,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":6.5,\"upper\":7.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"The federal policing order required fair and impartial justice, use-of-force data, early-warning systems, and stronger accountability procedures.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0033\",\"explanation\":\"The Bipartisan Safer Communities Act is a concrete, signed response to gun violence.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0035\",\"explanation\":\"The Emmett Till Antilynching Act is a major civil-rights protection step.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"The Violence Against Women Act reauthorization is a direct protection measure for survivors.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"Domestic Violence Awareness Month proclamation shows sustained executive attention to survivor protection.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"The hate-violence summit bundled policy, coordination, and public signaling into a prevention effort.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0039\",\"explanation\":\"The Election Threats Task Force is a concrete federal response to intimidation of election workers.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"Nationwide DOJ outreach on hate incidents broadened protection and prevention capacity.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0041\",\"explanation\":\"The Respect for Marriage Act strengthened family security and equal-protection status.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"Biden's January 6 remarks are reactive rather than proof of sustained end-state safety.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"Anti-political-violence rhetoric is important, but it does not itself fix the underlying threat environment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"Hate-crime data remained high in 2022, so the year was not a near-perfect safety environment.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"The United States inherited high levels of gun violence, hate crimes, and decentralized policing; the score reflects Biden's federal restraint and protection choices within those limits.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Strong for federal executive action and public leadership, but many domestic-safety outcomes are constrained by state and local control.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.4\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.6\",\"3B.7\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.3\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"January 6 rhetoric is condemnation and warning, not a completed remedy record.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"The anti-violence speech is valuable but still rhetorical.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"High hate-crime totals show the safety environment was still materially imperfect.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Primary presidential transcripts, DOJ materials, and official country-level reporting; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the judgment relies on the cited evidence and the embedded contrary notes.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Biden's score is solidly high because the record contains genuine federal protection and remedy efforts. The administration created policing accountability rules, signed major gun-safety and civil-rights laws, supported survivors of domestic violence, built hate-violence prevention infrastructure, and responded actively to election-worker intimidation. The main reason the score is not even higher is that the underlying violence environment remained severe, with hate crimes and gun violence still high and much of the system outside federal control. That means the year shows strong federal restraint and protection, but not exceptional transformation of the national safety landscape.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 5-6 because the file contains multiple concrete national protection measures and remedy structures, not just rhetoric or inherited baseline.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 8-10 because the underlying violence environment remained high and federal control is limited, so this is not a near-perfect safety record.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"recurring but not systematic state terror\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"failed-to-prevent some harms, but strongly remedial at the federal level\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"strong federal remedy orientation; implementation remains limited by federalism\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"open and highly visible\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"improved federal protection architecture without eliminating the underlying violence environment\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"moderate; direct for federal action, weaker for state/local violence outcomes\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.1-3B.3 are not the main story; the chapter turns on federal protection and remedy\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:VNM:19647\",\"iso3\":\"VNM\",\"ruler_id\":\"3411\",\"ruler_year_id\":19647,\"ruler_name\":\"Nguyễn Phú Trọng\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"3B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_3b_v4\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:3B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:CHN:15832\"],\"score_1_to_10\":2.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":87,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":2.0,\"upper\":3.0},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0021\",\"explanation\":\"Custody deaths, poor prison conditions, and limited accountability are clear negative facts.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0022\",\"explanation\":\"Indefinite house arrest and movement restrictions for activists show a tightly controlled civic space.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0023\",\"explanation\":\"Pham Doan Trang's conviction and pretrial treatment show how dissent was handled under the system.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0024\",\"explanation\":\"Environmental defenders were arrested and sentenced under tax-evasion charges, chilling advocacy.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0025\",\"explanation\":\"Amnesty's country-year synthesis describes a broad crackdown on dissent and at least 48 detained or sentenced activists and journalists.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0026\",\"explanation\":\"Detention abuse and a death in custody deepen the domestic-safety problem.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0027\",\"explanation\":\"Freedom House describes a one-party state with tightly restricted expression and assembly.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0028\",\"explanation\":\"RSF documents a controlled media landscape and widespread mistreatment of journalists.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"The five-year sentence for Le Anh Hung shows continued imprisonment of critical reporters.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"Le Van Dung's summary sentencing under anti-state propaganda charges reinforces the repression pattern.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"The repatriation of over 500 Vietnamese workers in Cambodia is a narrow protective action.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0032\",\"explanation\":\"The rescue/repatriation of trafficked citizens shows some outward-facing protection capacity.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Vietnam's one-party baseline makes a protective score difficult; the score reflects Trọng's maintenance of a closed political order in 2022, not every local custody event.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Strong system-level attribution through party-state control over police, prisons, and media, even where local officials carried out the specific repression.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"3B.1\",\"3B.2\",\"3B.4\",\"3B.5\",\"3B.8\",\"3B.9\",\"3B.10\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"3B.3\",\"3B.6\",\"3B.7\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"Rescuing and repatriating workers is a real but narrow protective step.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0032\",\"explanation\":\"The government did act to protect some citizens abroad, but that does not change the domestic repression record.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Official government reporting, UN/IGO, Amnesty, HRW, Freedom House, and RSF; no client matrix used.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were unavailable across the lens set, so the judgment rests on the cited evidence and the embedded contrary notes.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Vietnam's 2022 score is low because the record is dominated by political detention, prison abuse, house arrest, criminalization of dissent, and media control. The evidence is consistent across several independent source types and shows a closed one-party order that sharply constrains civic activity. There are a couple of narrow positive actions, such as repatriating workers from Cambodia, but they are outward-facing protection steps and do not offset the domestic repression picture. The score therefore sits in the severe-authoritarian band, above the absolute floor but clearly below any genuinely protective domestic-safety order.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 1-2 because the evidence is severe and system-wide, but it does not reach the historical mass-terror floor.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"I rejected 3+ because the 2022 record is not just selective abuse; it is a broad one-party repression system with weak remedy.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"severity_band\",\"value\":\"systematic repression\"},{\"field\":\"state_responsibility\",\"value\":\"direct/state-aligned\"},{\"field\":\"accountability_and_remedy\",\"value\":\"very weak; the party-state dominates and remedy is rare\"},{\"field\":\"information_environment\",\"value\":\"heavily constrained and censored\"},{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"continued repression with only narrow external protection actions\"},{\"field\":\"ruler_authority_and_attribution\",\"value\":\"very high system-level attribution through the party-state and security apparatus\"},{\"field\":\"lens_gaps\",\"value\":\"3B.3 is not the main driver; repression, detention, and media control are\"}]}],\"unavailable_dossiers\":[],\"batch_notes\":[\"The batch was calibrated on an absolute Chapter 3B scale, not on relative rank within the cohort.\",\"I used high-restraint comparators such as Germany, Japan, and the United States against severe-repression comparators such as Russia, Iran, China, and Vietnam to keep the scale from compressing around the midpoint.\",\"No dossier was forced to null because every projection supplied traceable in-dossier evidence and enough attribution detail for a defensible chapter judgment.\"],\"run_profile\":{\"provider_profile\":\"gpt-5.4-mini\",\"provider\":\"openai\",\"model\":\"gpt-5.4-mini\",\"dossier_count\":20,\"unavailable_dossier_count\":0,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":\"unknown_not_exposed_by_tool\",\"output_tokens\":\"unknown_not_exposed_by_tool\",\"total_tokens\":\"unknown_not_expo