{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"schema_version\":\"ruler_chapter_judgment_v1\",\"job_key\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"run_key\":\"2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1\",\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"target_year\":2022,\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"evaluations\":[{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:BGD:17068\",\"iso3\":\"BGD\",\"ruler_id\":\"2992\",\"ruler_year_id\":17068,\"ruler_name\":\"Sheikh Hasina\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":3.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.89,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":3.0,\"upper\":4.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0039\",\"explanation\":\"Shows the Hasina government mobilizing law-enforcement resources against opposition leaders and politically motivated charging patterns tied to the 2022 protest cycle.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0041\",\"explanation\":\"Documents repeated denial and confinement of opposition BNP gatherings and use of force against demonstrations, which is central to contestability and civic-space assessment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"Shows transnational repression and harassment of critics abroad and relatives at home, extending political control beyond ordinary domestic policing.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0045\",\"explanation\":\"Reports a December 2022 crackdown that produced arrests and deaths around an opposition rally, reinforcing the pattern of coercive suppression.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0050\",\"explanation\":\"Describes hostile media conditions and abuse of the Digital Security Act that constrained journalists and critics in the governing environment.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0051\",\"explanation\":\"The law minister's rejection of restoring the caretaker system shows the government closing off a major transfer-rule demand from the opposition.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0053\",\"explanation\":\"Even when permission was granted, it came with heavy conditions and unequal treatment of opposition versus ruling-party rallies, which tempers a still-formal but constrained political arena.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Bangladesh retained formal elections, civilian control, and some procedural opposition space, but those institutions were heavily constrained in practice by Hasina's governing coalition, police, legal harassment, and media pressure.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mostly indirect but strong: the evidence ties repression to the Hasina government, senior police, ministers, and Awami League-aligned actors rather than to a single personal command transcript.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.2 electoral manipulation/entrenchment\",\"4B.3 toleration of opposition/protest\",\"4B.6 media and information control\",\"4B.8 transfer rules\",\"4B.9 surveillance/harassment\",\"4B.10 erosive trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.1 meaningful contestability is only partly evidenced by formal procedures\",\"4B.4 independent-oversight strengthening is thin\",\"4B.5 personality-cult/loyalty-test evidence is limited\",\"4B.7 equality protection evidence is indirect\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0051\",\"explanation\":\"Shows the government relying on court doctrine and constitutional language, which keeps the score above the hardest authoritarian floor.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0053\",\"explanation\":\"Shows some opposition rally permission, but only under restrictive conditions, so it moderates rather than reverses the negative picture.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Mostly official U.S. government reporting plus HRW, RSF, Amnesty, Internet Society, and local Bangladesh media. The mix is strong on repression and media control, but direct personal-command proof is thinner than state-level attribution.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Sheikh Hasina's 2022 record sits well below a free or meaningfully contestable system. The strongest evidence shows the government using law-enforcement, rally restrictions, the Digital Security Act, and transnational pressure to narrow opposition space, while a caretaker-government restoration was explicitly rejected. Those facts matter because they go to the core of whether citizens, opposition parties, and independent media could still compete on roughly equal terms. At the same time, Bangladesh was not a fully sealed one-party system: formal elections, civilian control, and occasional permission for opposition activity remained, so the chapter does not fall to the very lowest anchor. The evidence therefore supports a score in the low end of the mixed-authoritarian range rather than a materially higher one.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would overstate closure. The dossier still shows formal electoral institutions, civilian rule, and some limited permission channels rather than total elimination of political choice.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score is not defensible because the year shows sustained repression of opposition, media, and digital space, plus active resistance to restoring a neutral caretaker election mechanism.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Erosive: formal electoral structures remained, but day-to-day political freedom weakened through coercive administration, media control, and transfer-rule resistance.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:BRA:19646\",\"iso3\":\"BRA\",\"ruler_id\":\"3413\",\"ruler_year_id\":19646,\"ruler_name\":\"Jair Bolsonaro\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":6.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.9,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":5.5,\"upper\":6.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0043\",\"explanation\":\"The election authority's result confirms a competitive election and an actual transfer-of-power outcome rather than a closed-authoritarian entrenchment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"The TSE's authenticity test and public voting safeguards support a still-functioning electoral system despite Bolsonaro's fraud narrative.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0047\",\"explanation\":\"The TSE's post-election rebuttal of fake news shows institutional resistance to Bolsonaro-linked disinformation, which kept contestability alive.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0045\",\"explanation\":\"Freedom House describes Bolsonaro and allies flooding the campaign with disinformation, which materially degraded the information environment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0044\",\"explanation\":\"The TSE's prior rebuttal to Bolsonaro's fraud claims is relevant as context for his repeated attacks on electoral legitimacy, though the institution held.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Brazil entered 2022 with competitive elections and resilient institutions, and Bolsonaro governed within a system that still forced a real transfer outcome. His conduct degraded the information environment, but he did not successfully break the electoral framework.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mixed but meaningful: the evidence links Bolsonaro directly to fraud rhetoric and disinformation, but many countervailing facts are institutional rather than personal.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.1 contestability\",\"4B.2 anti-entrenchment\",\"4B.8 transfer rules\",\"4B.10 overall trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.3 opposition tolerance is mixed because the dossier centers on disinformation rather than direct opposition bans\",\"4B.4 oversight-strengthening evidence is limited\",\"4B.5 loyalty-test/personality-cult evidence is indirect\",\"4B.6 media freedom is harmed but not fully closed\",\"4B.7 equality protections are not the main issue here\",\"4B.9 digital controls are present but less central than disinformation\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0043\",\"explanation\":\"The election result and turnover undercut any claim that Bolsonaro successfully closed the system.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"Institutional audit procedures remained public and operational, which limits the score from falling into the authoritarian range.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Primary election-authority records plus Freedom House and Reuters-based reporting. The strongest evidence is institutional and official, while the main negative evidence is Bolsonaro-linked disinformation and fraud rhetoric.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Jair Bolsonaro's 2022 record is mixed and clearly erosive, but not successfully authoritarian. He helped poison the information environment with disinformation and fraud claims, and he did so against a backdrop of repeated institutional conflict with the election system. However, Brazil still held a competitive election, the election authority publicly audited the process, and power actually transferred after Bolsonaro lost. That combination keeps the score above the genuinely backsliding or semi-closed regimes, but below the high-trust democratic anchors. The evidence supports a middling score because Bolsonaro weakened contestability without extinguishing it.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would ignore the fact that the election still produced an opposition victory and that electoral institutions held up under pressure.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would overstate restraint. Bolsonaro repeatedly attacked electoral legitimacy and amplified disinformation, which materially damaged political freedom even though institutions survived.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Mixed and erosive: democratic turnover survived, but Bolsonaro's disinformation campaign and legitimacy attacks weakened confidence in contestability.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:CHN:15832\",\"iso3\":\"CHN\",\"ruler_id\":\"1432\",\"ruler_year_id\":15832,\"ruler_name\":\"Xi Jinping\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":1.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.97,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":1.0,\"upper\":2.0},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0052\",\"explanation\":\"The COVID-curb loosening after protests shows some limited responsiveness to public pressure, though it does not amount to meaningful political openness.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0054\",\"explanation\":\"Xi's own Xinjiang remarks make the centralization and paternalistic security framing explicit, which helps identify his direct leadership role rather than merely anonymous bureaucracy.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0044\",\"explanation\":\"The party congress consolidated Xi's core position and rallied the party, military, and people around him, reflecting not contestability but entrenchment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0045\",\"explanation\":\"Reuters reports Xi securing a third term with loyalists, which is strong evidence of elite closure rather than free leadership turnover.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0053\",\"explanation\":\"The UN OHCHR assessment of serious violations in Xinjiang and possible crimes against humanity is decisive negative evidence for political freedom under Xi's rule.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0059\",\"explanation\":\"The U.S. country report on Hong Kong documents coercive closure of media and protest space, showing that freedom was not merely restricted but systematically narrowed.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"China in 2022 remained a one-party system with party control over elections, appointments, security, and information. Xi's authority was highly centralized and the institutional baseline itself was authoritarian, so the judge is evaluating a closed system with only minimal outward responsiveness.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Direct and system-level: the evidence connects Xi personally to core party-state consolidation and to Xinjiang policy framing, while many abuses are carried out through the security apparatus he leads.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.2 entrenchment through party-state control\",\"4B.3 repression of opposition, protest, and journalism\",\"4B.5 loyalty and personality-centered consolidation\",\"4B.6 censorship and information control\",\"4B.7 repression of minorities and Hong Kong/Tibet dissent\",\"4B.8 succession and term-limit breach\",\"4B.9 surveillance and digital control\",\"4B.10 fully authoritarian trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.1 no meaningful free competition exists, so the evidence is mostly about its absence rather than a clean election test\",\"4B.4 independent oversight is so weak that affirmative strengthening evidence is scarce\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0052\",\"explanation\":\"COVID policy loosening after protest pressure is the only notable responsiveness, but it does not materially change the political-freedom score.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0054\",\"explanation\":\"Direct Xi remarks on Xinjiang show leadership intent, but they do not mitigate the repression record.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"A dense mix of official PRC materials, UN/OHCHR findings, U.S. government reporting, Reuters, HRW, Amnesty, RSF, and CPJ. The mix is strong for documenting repression from multiple angles, but personal command evidence is still partly system-level.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Xi Jinping's 2022 record is an archetype of the closed-authoritarian end of the scale. The strongest evidence shows consolidation of his core position, third-term entrenchment, mass repression in Xinjiang, and severe media and protest suppression in Hong Kong and elsewhere. The only meaningful counterweight is limited policy responsiveness after the late-2022 protest wave, but that is not political freedom and does not restore contestability, independent oversight, or equal civic space. A score near the floor is therefore warranted because the regime systemically eliminates meaningful competition and severely suppresses dissent.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score is unavailable in the chapter rubric; the record is already at the practical floor for systematic authoritarian closure.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would be indefensible because the evidence shows entrenched party-state control, repression of minorities, and no real electoral or institutional contestability.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Authoritarian consolidation: the party-state tightened control over elections, information, minority regions, and elite succession.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:COD:16113\",\"iso3\":\"COD\",\"ruler_id\":\"2830\",\"ruler_year_id\":16113,\"ruler_name\":\"Félix Tshisekedi\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":4.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.86,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":3.0,\"upper\":5.0},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0055\",\"explanation\":\"The U.S. country report says the government did not restrict or disrupt internet access, which is a notable counterpoint against a fully closed information environment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0048\",\"explanation\":\"The electoral-law debate and opposition boycott show a contested but still functioning political arena rather than total one-party closure.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0001\",\"explanation\":\"The official biography is only an identity baseline, but it confirms the office holder and the relevant 2022 authority context.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0051\",\"explanation\":\"Amnesty says the state of siege was used to silence critics through arbitrary detention, military-court prosecutions, and violent suppression of protest.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0052\",\"explanation\":\"Authorities threatened to crush protests and used blanket bans and arrests under the emergency regime to suppress accountability and dissent.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0053\",\"explanation\":\"HRW reports military authorities curtailing expression, breaking up peaceful demonstrations with lethal force, and arbitrarily detaining activists, journalists, and opposition members.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0059\",\"explanation\":\"Police arrests, detention, and beatings of journalists covering an opposition protest show persistent coercive pressure on civic space.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"The DRC's inherited context is weak institutions, eastern conflict, and recurring security-emergency governance. Tshisekedi did not preside over a closed one-party system, but the emergency regime substantially narrowed civic freedom and accountability in 2022.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mixed and mostly system-level: the evidence links abuses to Tshisekedi's declared and extended state of siege and to state forces under his administration, but many incidents are provincial or military-led rather than personally ordered.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.2 election-law and institutional manipulation concerns\",\"4B.3 protest and civil-society repression\",\"4B.6 journalist intimidation and censorship\",\"4B.8 a still-contested election environment\",\"4B.9 administrative harassment through security forces\",\"4B.10 negative 2022 trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.1 free contestability is only indirectly shown\",\"4B.4 independent oversight strengthening is thin\",\"4B.5 personality-cult evidence is absent\",\"4B.7 equality protection evidence is not well developed\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0055\",\"explanation\":\"The absence of internet disruption is a meaningful restraint and keeps the score above the most repressive end.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0048\",\"explanation\":\"The electoral-law debate shows some institutional contestation rather than total closure.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Mostly Amnesty, HRW, CPJ, Reuters, and U.S. government reporting, plus one official office biography and a Congolese media report. The mix is strong on coercive state behavior, but command responsibility is often indirect or provincial.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Félix Tshisekedi's 2022 record is mixed but clearly authoritarian in important pockets. The state of siege and related security practices narrowed protest space, chilled criticism, and produced arrests, prosecutions, and lethal force against demonstrators and journalists. Yet the dossier also shows some open political contestation, an intact internet environment, and debate over electoral law rather than complete closure. That places Tshisekedi in the lower-middle band: serious freedom loss, but not the total elimination associated with the hardest authoritarian cases.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would ignore the remaining political contestation, the lack of total information blackout, and the fact that not all coercion was centrally documented as personal presidential command.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would overstate openness. The emergency regime and recurring abuse against critics and journalists materially undercut political freedom and accountability.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Erosive and security-driven: emergency governance narrowed freedom even as some electoral and institutional contestation remained.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"iso3\":\"DEU\",\"ruler_id\":\"2975\",\"ruler_year_id\":16984,\"ruler_name\":\"Olaf Scholz\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":8.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.89,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":7.5,\"upper\":8.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0033\",\"explanation\":\"The refugee-support decision extends concrete rights and services, showing affirmative executive use of power to protect a vulnerable group.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0039\",\"explanation\":\"The constitutional court's press-freedom ruling confirms robust judicial independence and a strong media-rights baseline under Scholz's chancellorship.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0041\",\"explanation\":\"The parliamentary-question ruling shows that oversight bodies could force executive disclosure, an important sign of institutional constraint.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"The cabinet's draft law on democratic participation and extremism prevention is an affirmative attempt to support pluralism and resilience.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0043\",\"explanation\":\"Scholz's Chanukka speech is symbolic, but it is an explicit public commitment to minority protection and anti-antisemitism.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"The ECHR judgment on the failed independent investigation into racial profiling shows that some accountability gaps remained.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"The constitutional court's surveillance-data ruling shows the state still needed judicial correction on proportionality and clarity.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0032\",\"explanation\":\"The commemorative speech is not operational reform; it signals commitment but does not by itself prove wider institutional change.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Germany began with very strong constitutional restraint, independent courts, and protected elections. Scholz largely governed within and reinforced that framework rather than trying to override it.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mostly direct at the cabinet and chancellery level, with much of the strongest evidence coming from courts and institutions that constrained Scholz rather than from personal directives alone.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.3 tolerance of criticism, protest, and civic activity\",\"4B.4 strengthening of courts, parliament, and oversight\",\"4B.6 press and information protections\",\"4B.7 minority protection and anti-antisemitism stance\",\"4B.8 transfer and constitutional restraint\",\"4B.10 strong positive trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.1 contestability is inherited more than newly created\",\"4B.2 manipulation/entrenchment evidence is mostly absent because the dossier is positive overall\",\"4B.5 personality-cult or loyalty-test evidence is weak\",\"4B.9 surveillance control is constrained but still needs judicial correction\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0029\",\"explanation\":\"Shows a real rights-remedy failure, so the score cannot reach the exceptional top of the scale.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"Judicial correction of surveillance law indicates residual problems, even though the courts worked.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Mostly primary German constitutional and federal materials, plus official reports and a small amount of Reuters/parliamentary coverage. The mix is strong on actual institutional outputs, not just stated intent.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Olaf Scholz's 2022 Germany is a high-freedom case. Independent courts constrained the executive, parliament could force disclosure, media and assembly rights remained robust, and the government took affirmative steps to support refugees and democratic resilience. The main negative items are real but limited: a discriminatory-policing accountability gap, a surveillance-law correction, and the fact that some favorable evidence is symbolic rather than transformative. That puts Scholz well above the midrange, but not at the extreme top reserved for unusually consequential self-binding or freedom expansion under direct pressure. The evidence supports a strong score because political freedom was durable, legally protected, and mostly reinforced rather than eroded.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would understate the strength of German courts, parliamentary oversight, and the broad freedom of assembly and expression visible in the dossier.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would require more exceptional freedom expansion or self-binding under pressure than the 2022 record shows; the key negatives were corrected, but not in a way that justifies the very top anchor.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Strongly positive and institutionally reinforced: Scholz governed within a resilient constitutional order and made some affirmative pluralist moves.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:EGY:16232\",\"iso3\":\"EGY\",\"ruler_id\":\"2851\",\"ruler_year_id\":16232,\"ruler_name\":\"Abdel Fattah el-Sisi\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":2.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.95,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":1.5,\"upper\":2.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"The national dialogue beginning and the participation of civil society and opposition members provide a narrow but real signal of consultative space.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0043\",\"explanation\":\"The planned dialogue and protest-law discussion show that the state was at least speaking the language of controlled political opening.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0039\",\"explanation\":\"Freedom House says Sisi governed in an increasingly authoritarian manner with virtually nonexistent opposition and tightly restricted dissent, press freedom, and assembly.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0044\",\"explanation\":\"The U.S. government report details severe restrictions on expression, media, internet freedom, assembly, and political participation.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0045\",\"explanation\":\"Amnesty says the human-rights strategy was propaganda cover while authorities censored websites, raided media, and arbitrarily detained thousands.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"Hundreds of arrests around COP27 demonstrate that protest-related coercion remained active in 2022.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0047\",\"explanation\":\"HRW reports nearly 700 detentions tied to protest calls and surveillance around COP27, which is strong evidence of sustained repression.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"The appointment of a military general to a key judicial post signals continued executive politicization of institutions.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Egypt in 2022 was already a highly repressive presidential system with weak opposition, heavy security control, and constrained media. Sisi's rule retained that baseline and in several respects deepened it.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Direct and strong: the dossier ties the repression, judicial politicization, and controlled dialogue to Sisi's administration and appointment powers, even when individual operations were carried out by security agencies.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.2 entrenchment through appointments and control\",\"4B.3 repression of opposition and protest\",\"4B.4 politicized institutions\",\"4B.6 media and internet repression\",\"4B.7 weak equality and civic protection\",\"4B.9 security-state harassment\",\"4B.10 heavily negative trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.1 contestability is nearly absent and mostly shown through retrospective reports\",\"4B.5 loyalty-cult evidence is present but not the central question\",\"4B.8 succession rules are less directly evidenced in 2022 than general authoritarian entrenchment\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"The national dialogue is the only meaningful mitigating item, but it is too narrow and controlled to offset the repression record.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0043\",\"explanation\":\"Consultative language does not equal political opening; it sits alongside extensive coercion.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Mostly Freedom House, U.S. government, Amnesty, HRW, and Reuters. The mix is strong and mutually reinforcing on repression, with some official government rhetoric that is clearly outweighed by rights evidence.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Abdel Fattah el-Sisi's 2022 Egypt remains a classic low-freedom case. Opposition was virtually nonexistent, media and internet space were tightly restricted, and COP27 saw mass arrests and surveillance around protest calls. The national dialogue and a few consultative gestures do not materially change that picture because they were tightly managed and did not produce visible pluralism or independent constraint. A score near the bottom is therefore justified, though not the absolute floor, because the system still retained some formal institutions and occasional controlled dialogue rather than total closure.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would overstate closure relative to the rubric floor, because some formal institutions and limited consultative activity still existed.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score is not defensible because the evidence shows pervasive repression across speech, assembly, media, and electoral participation, with executive politicization of institutions.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Deeply authoritarian and stable: managed consultation coexisted with sustained repression and institutional capture.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:ETH:16782\",\"iso3\":\"ETH\",\"ruler_id\":\"2310\",\"ruler_year_id\":16782,\"ruler_name\":\"Abiy Ahmed\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":3.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.87,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":2.5,\"upper\":4.0},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"The National Dialogue Commission and civil-society partnership show a formal institutional attempt at political inclusion.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"The blocked CSO press event still resulted in a published peace statement, which is a narrow sign that civic actors were not fully silenced.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"Amnesty says federal authorities restricted aid to Tigray, arbitrarily detained thousands, and eroded freedom of expression in 2022.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0039\",\"explanation\":\"The expulsion of a correspondent shows direct press restriction by state authorities under the Abiy government.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"The internet shutdown in Tigray remained in force through most of 2022, severely restricting access and accountability.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0097\",\"explanation\":\"Freedom House links emergency powers to crackdowns on media and journalists, confirming that restrictions were not isolated.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0098\",\"explanation\":\"Access Now's two-year shutdown documentation underscores the breadth and duration of digital control.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Ethiopia in 2022 was shaped by war, emergency powers, and severe security pressures. Abiy's government had some reformist and consultative language, but the practical environment remained coercive and information-restricted.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mixed but substantial: the evidence often attributes actions to federal security authorities and emergency powers under Abiy's government, even when it lacks a personal order transcript.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.3 repression of dissent and journalism\",\"4B.6 media and internet restrictions\",\"4B.9 shutdowns and administrative harassment\",\"4B.10 negative trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.1 free contestability is largely absent as a meaningful test\",\"4B.4 independent-oversight strengthening is weak\",\"4B.5 personality/loyalty evidence is not central\",\"4B.7 equality protection evidence is sparse\",\"4B.8 succession-rule evidence is not relevant in 2022\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"The National Dialogue Commission partnership is the main mitigating fact, but it does not overcome the coercive record.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"The published peace statement shows partial civic space, not durable freedom.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Amnesty, Access Now, CPJ, Freedom House, and local reporting. The evidence is consistent on repression and digital controls, with formal dialogue efforts providing only limited counterweight.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Abiy Ahmed's 2022 Ethiopia is significantly constrained and coercive, though not as sealed as the hardest authoritarian cases. The clearest negative facts are aid restrictions in Tigray, arrests and press expulsions, and a long-running shutdown that cut off communication and accountability. The dialogue commission and civil-society partnership are real but too thin to counterbalance the broader pattern of emergency rule and information control. The score therefore sits in the lower-middle range: serious political-freedom erosion, but still some formal institutional movement rather than total closure.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would ignore the partial consultative institutions and the fact that the system was coercive but not fully closed in the same way as the hardest authoritarian cases.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would overread the dialogue and partnership gestures. The operative reality was still detentions, shutdowns, and press restrictions under emergency powers.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Erosive under emergency and conflict conditions: consultation existed on paper, but coercive control dominated practice.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IDN:16199\",\"iso3\":\"IDN\",\"ruler_id\":\"2636\",\"ruler_year_id\":16199,\"ruler_name\":\"Joko Widodo\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":6.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.84,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":5.5,\"upper\":6.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"Jokowi's public rejection of term-extension talk is direct evidence that he did not try to suspend the constitutional election timetable in 2022.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"The KPU's 2024 election timetable being formally adopted shows the constitutional succession calendar remained intact under his presidency.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0043\",\"explanation\":\"The KPU's public date-setting and stage launch support a functioning electoral sequence rather than a closed transfer process.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"The recall-vote process and its official count show a real, rule-bound contestability mechanism in the Jokowi era.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0047\",\"explanation\":\"Kominfo's platform-registration and blocking threats expanded administrative control over online speech.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0048\",\"explanation\":\"Amnesty's account of intimidation of journalists shows that press freedom remained vulnerable under the wider political environment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0049\",\"explanation\":\"Amnesty documents a year-end shrinkage in civil liberties, use of the ITE law, and repression in Papua, all of which undercut the freedom score.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0050\",\"explanation\":\"The new criminal code added speech and insult offenses, including complaint-triggered provisions involving the president, which worsened the legal environment for dissent.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Indonesia remained a competitive electoral democracy with institutional procedures and a constitutional term limit, but also with persistent media pressure, digital control, and uneven civil-liberties enforcement.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Direct on the term-extension episode and indirect on broader administrative controls. Some negative evidence is government-level rather than personally traced to Jokowi, but it still falls under his presidency.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.1 contestability\",\"4B.2 rejection of overt term manipulation\",\"4B.8 transfer rules\",\"4B.9 administrative digital controls\",\"4B.10 mixed trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.4 oversight-strengthening evidence is limited\",\"4B.5 personality-cult evidence is not central\",\"4B.6 media freedom is constrained but not shut down\",\"4B.7 equality protection evidence is mixed and mostly structural\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"His public rejection of term extension is a meaningful restraint and keeps the score above the more erosive cases.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"The recall process shows that institutional contestability still functioned in 2022.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Primarily official Indonesian election records and presidential statements, supplemented by Freedom House, Amnesty, and Reuters-based reporting. The mix is good for transfer rules and digital governance, weaker on personal intent behind every restriction.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Joko Widodo's 2022 Indonesia is a mixed case: it remained electorally competitive and he publicly rejected talk of extending his term, but media pressure, digital controls, and speech criminalization continued to narrow freedom. The constitutional timetable and recall process show that democratic transfer rules still had force. However, the new criminal code, platform controls, and documented civil-liberties shrinkage show a real erosive trend rather than simple neutrality. The score is therefore in the midrange, reflecting both preserved contestability and meaningful backsliding pressures.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would understate the fact that elections, recall mechanisms, and term limits still worked in 2022 and that Jokowi publicly rejected extension talk.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would ignore the cumulative effect of digital controls, speech offenses, and the broader shrinkage in civil liberties documented for 2022.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Mixed but mildly erosive: the election calendar and term limits held, yet speech and digital controls tightened.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\",\"iso3\":\"IND\",\"ruler_id\":\"1517\",\"ruler_year_id\":19645,\"ruler_name\":\"Narendra Modi\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":5.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.83,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":4.5,\"upper\":5.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"The recall and other constitutional/electoral procedures show that large-scale contestability remained in place, even under pressure.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0050\",\"explanation\":\"Party guidance to exercise caution on religion after the Prophet Mohammed controversy shows crisis containment rather than immediate escalation.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0044\",\"explanation\":\"Access Now's count of at least 84 shutdowns in 2022 is strong evidence of administrative limits on political and civic space.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0045\",\"explanation\":\"HRW's account of broader crackdowns on activists, media, and minorities shows a sustained erosion pattern.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"RSF's reporting on refusals and access restrictions for foreign correspondents shows pressure on independent journalism.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0047\",\"explanation\":\"Amnesty's submission on laws threatening expression and assembly indicates legal backsliding rather than neutral governance.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0049\",\"explanation\":\"The U.S. religious-freedom report's account of punitive state and local abuses against Muslims shows weak protection of unpopular groups.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0024\",\"explanation\":\"ARTICLE 19's quantified attack totals and stigmatizing executive comments are strong evidence that the information environment was hostile.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"India remained a competitive democracy with real elections and open institutional dispute, but 2022 featured substantial pressure on media, minorities, and digital space. Modi governed within a system that still allowed contestation, yet his administration contributed to a harsher civic environment.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mixed: some evidence directly traces to Modi's rhetoric and government actions, while other parts are broader state or party-environment patterns rather than personal orders.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.1 contestability survives in a competitive democracy\",\"4B.2 rule manipulation and executive pressure\",\"4B.3 pressure on activists, journalists, and minorities\",\"4B.6 media and internet control\",\"4B.7 political equality for minorities\",\"4B.9 shutdowns and digital controls\",\"4B.10 mildly erosive trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.4 oversight-strengthening evidence is limited\",\"4B.5 personality-cult evidence is not the main issue\",\"4B.8 transfer rules are intact and thus not a central negative in the dossier\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"The recall process and regular electoral institutions keep India well above the low-authoritarian anchor.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0050\",\"explanation\":\"The ruling party's cautionary response to communal tensions shows some awareness of restraint, though it does not outweigh the abuse record.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"A strong mix of official election and government records plus ARTICLE 19, HRW, RSF, Amnesty, USCIRF, and UN reporting. The evidence is broad and consistent, though direct attribution to Modi personally is often indirect rather than documentary.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Narendra Modi's 2022 India remains a competitive democracy, but it is not politically free in a clean sense. The country still had real electoral mechanisms and a functioning recall-style contestability signal, yet the year also showed frequent internet shutdowns, legal pressure on speech, hostility to independent journalism, and discriminatory or punitive treatment of minorities. That combination places the score in the middle of the chapter scale: meaningfully democratic, but with serious erosive pressure on political freedom. The evidence supports a midpoint because the state was not closed, yet freedom was plainly diminished.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would miss the endurance of electoral competition and the fact that the regime still allowed meaningful public opposition and institutional dispute.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would understate the breadth of repression, especially around internet shutdowns, press pressure, and minority-targeted state action.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Mixed and erosive: democratic competition endured, but legal, digital, and communal pressure narrowed freedom.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IRN:19648\",\"iso3\":\"IRN\",\"ruler_id\":\"3412\",\"ruler_year_id\":19648,\"ruler_name\":\"Ali Khamenei\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":1.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.98,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":1.0,\"upper\":2.0},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0001\",\"explanation\":\"The constitutional text establishes Khamenei's formal command over the armed forces and war-and-peace powers, which is necessary context for evaluating his responsibility.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0025\",\"explanation\":\"Amnesty's leaked-document account shows top-level orders to brutally confront protesters, a decisive sign of coercive closure.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0032\",\"explanation\":\"The death-penalty campaign against protesters demonstrates systematic intimidation through the judiciary and security apparatus.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"The UN Human Rights Council's account of hundreds killed and thousands arrested in the crackdown is strong evidence of severe repression.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"CPJ's report of journalist arrests amid blackouts shows direct repression of information space.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0041\",\"explanation\":\"OHCHR's account of live fire, detentions, and harsh treatment of women protesting hijab enforcement documents coercive rule rather than freedom.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0044\",\"explanation\":\"NetBlocks' network-disruption evidence shows the state used digital controls to suppress protest and coordination.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"Khamenei's own public backing of security forces and call for harsh prosecution is direct ruler endorsement of repression.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0048\",\"explanation\":\"The Zahedan crackdown report is decisive for minority-region repression and mass lethal force.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Iran in 2022 was a tightly controlled theocratic-authoritarian system with no meaningful free competition. Khamenei's formal powers and de facto authority cover the security, judiciary, and political order, so the baseline itself is closed and coercive.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Strong and partly direct: some evidence is Khamenei's own rhetoric, while much of the abuse is system-level conduct by institutions under his authority.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.2 entrenchment and security control\",\"4B.3 repression of protest, journalism, and civil society\",\"4B.5 coercive loyalty and militia celebration\",\"4B.6 censorship, internet blackouts, and media suppression\",\"4B.7 minority repression\",\"4B.9 digital controls and harassment\",\"4B.10 fully negative trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.1 contestability is effectively absent\",\"4B.4 oversight institutions are not meaningfully independent\",\"4B.8 transfer rules are not the relevant test in a noncompetitive system\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0001\",\"explanation\":\"Formal constitutional divisions exist, but they do not produce real contestability or independent constraint in practice.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"Khamenei expressed personal sorrow over Amini's death, but that does not mitigate the broader repression record.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"A strong mix of constitutional text, Amnesty, UN/OHCHR, CPJ, NetBlocks, Reuters, and other human-rights reporting. The sources converge on systematic coercion, with direct personal attribution strongest for Khamenei's backing rhetoric and weaker for individual operational orders.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Ali Khamenei's 2022 Iran is one of the clearest low-end cases in the batch. The evidence shows lethal protest repression, mass arrests, internet disruption, death-penalty intimidation, and public endorsement of security-force crackdowns. Whatever formal constitutional language exists, it does not create meaningful contestability or independent constraint. The score is therefore at the floor of the severe-authoritarian range, with only minimal complexity coming from the distinction between Khamenei's direct rhetoric and the broader security apparatus acting under his authority.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score is unavailable in practical terms; the dossier already shows the maximum closure and coercion this rubric can represent.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would be incompatible with the repeated evidence of lethal repression, internet blackout, and judicial intimidation of protesters and journalists.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Severely authoritarian and coercive: the crackdown on protests and information space dominated the year.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:JPN:16638\",\"iso3\":\"JPN\",\"ruler_id\":\"2895\",\"ruler_year_id\":16638,\"ruler_name\":\"Fumio Kishida\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":7.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.9,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":7.0,\"upper\":8.0},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0034\",\"explanation\":\"The annual rights report says there were no arbitrary killings, disappearances, or credible torture reports, and no political prisoners, which is a strong baseline for political freedom.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0035\",\"explanation\":\"Freedom of speech, assembly, association, and NGO activity remained generally respected, with peaceful demonstrations frequent.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0041\",\"explanation\":\"The 2022 upper house election was held on schedule with normal competitive results and a retained coalition majority.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"Legislative elections remained free and fair in broad terms, with fair campaign rules and only a narrower issue of malapportionment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0049\",\"explanation\":\"Kishida ordered a church-links investigation and apologized, which is a real accountability response to a political-trust crisis.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"RSF's account of kisha-club self-censorship and the Secrets Law shows a real but structural pressure on journalism.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"Pre-indictment detention and ethnic profiling concerns show due-process weaknesses that prevent a top score.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0048\",\"explanation\":\"The Unification Church reshuffle did not fully purge problematic ties, which shows imperfect political cleansing.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0050\",\"explanation\":\"Public support for the cabinet fell sharply amid the church scandal, reflecting trust damage that the administration had not fully repaired.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Japan entered 2022 as a very strong constitutional democracy with independent courts, routine elections, and broad civic freedom. Kishida mostly inherited and maintained that baseline, while facing residual media and due-process issues.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mostly indirect and institutional: the strongest evidence reflects a resilient system under Kishida rather than major personal attempts to override it, though he did make some direct corrective moves on the church scandal.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.1 contestability through routine elections\",\"4B.3 tolerance of protest and civic space\",\"4B.4 judicial and parliamentary independence\",\"4B.7 equal political rights baseline\",\"4B.8 transfer rules\",\"4B.10 stable positive trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.2 manipulation/entrenchment evidence is limited\",\"4B.5 personality-cult evidence is not central\",\"4B.6 media pressure exists but is structural rather than overtly coercive\",\"4B.9 surveillance/shutdown evidence is limited\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"Due-process and profiling concerns show that Japan is not perfect and prevent a 9 or 10.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"Structural media self-censorship remains a meaningful weakness even in a high-freedom system.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Mostly official U.S. government and Japanese or international legal/election materials, supplemented by Reuters, Kyodo, and RSF/Reuters Institute reporting. The mix is strong on institutions and elections, with some structural criticism of media and detention practices.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Fumio Kishida's 2022 Japan is a high-freedom case. Elections were regular and competitive, courts and parliament remained independent enough to constrain the executive, and civic space was broadly open. The main blemishes are structural media self-censorship, some due-process concerns, and the incomplete cleanup of church-linked patronage networks. Those issues matter, but they do not pull Japan out of the high range because political choice, independent institutions, and transfer rules remained strong. The score is therefore comfortably above the midrange but short of the very top because the record shows resilience rather than exceptional new strengthening under pressure.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would ignore the depth of Japan's institutional freedoms and the fact that elections, courts, and protest rights remained robust throughout 2022.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would require more than a stable baseline and reactive cleanup; the dossier does not show exceptional expansion of pluralism under direct threat.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Stable and high-freedom: Kishida governed within a durable democratic framework with only limited structural frictions.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:MEX:15801\",\"iso3\":\"MEX\",\"ruler_id\":\"2779\",\"ruler_year_id\":15801,\"ruler_name\":\"Andrés Manuel López Obrador\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":5.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.84,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":5.0,\"upper\":6.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"The recall process and its official count show that a meaningful constitutional contestability mechanism remained live in 2022.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"AMLO's own electoral-reform proposal can be read as a stated attempt to justify clean elections, though it was politically contested and not necessarily freedom-enhancing in effect.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"His public denial of reelection intent matters because it directly addresses transfer-rule concerns, even if self-denial is not proof of compliance.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0024\",\"explanation\":\"ARTICLE 19's 2022 attack count, including state-actor involvement and stigmatizing executive comments, is strong evidence of a hostile media environment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"The UN human-rights statement on illicit surveillance against journalists and defenders shows state pressure on criticism.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0039\",\"explanation\":\"Congress rejected AMLO's controversial electoral reform, which shows the executive sought rule changes that many saw as threatening independence even though the legislature blocked them.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0041\",\"explanation\":\"The hostile morning briefing style singled out journalists and publications, which is a ruler-linked pressure pattern.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"The 2022 press-attack totals and stigmatizing executive comments are strong evidence that political and media space was meaningfully degraded.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0044\",\"explanation\":\"UN OHCHR concern about illegal surveillance reinforces that the pressure was not merely rhetorical.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Mexico retained real electoral competition and a constitutional transfer framework, but the AMLO administration exerted heavy rhetorical and administrative pressure on media and critics. The system was open enough to resist his electoral reform, yet not free of executive intimidation.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mixed: some evidence is directly AMLO's own rhetoric and proposals, while other evidence shows state or surveillance behavior under his administration without a clean personal-order transcript.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.1 contestability\",\"4B.2 attempted entrenchment and rule pressure\",\"4B.3 pressure on critics and journalists\",\"4B.6 media freedom and surveillance\",\"4B.8 transfer rules\",\"4B.9 administrative harassment\",\"4B.10 mildly erosive trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.4 oversight-strengthening evidence is limited\",\"4B.5 personality-cult evidence is not central\",\"4B.7 political equality evidence is secondary\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"The recall process shows that democratic contestability still functioned, preventing a lower authoritarian score.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0039\",\"explanation\":\"Congress rejecting the reform shows institutional resistance, which moderates the score upward.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"A mix of official election and presidential records, Reuters, ARTICLE 19, UN human-rights reporting, and media/journalism research. The evidence is broad and credible, though some of the strongest abuse claims are indirect or system-level rather than personally ordered.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Andrés Manuel López Obrador's 2022 Mexico is a mixed democracy with real contestability but clear executive pressure. The recall process and legislative resistance show that the system could still constrain him, and he did not eliminate transfer rules. Yet the record also shows a hostile media environment, surveillance concerns, stigmatizing executive comments, and an attempted electoral reform that critics believed would weaken independence. That makes the score squarely midrange: not an authoritarian slide, but definitely not a clean freedom-preserving presidency.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would miss the fact that electoral contestability and legislative checks actually worked in 2022.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would ignore the scale of media hostility, surveillance concerns, and the attempted institutional redesign around elections.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Mixed and mildly erosive: democratic institutions held, but executive rhetoric and pressure narrowed freedom.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:NGA:15787\",\"iso3\":\"NGA\",\"ruler_id\":\"2776\",\"ruler_year_id\":15787,\"ruler_name\":\"Muhammadu Buhari\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":5.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.83,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":4.5,\"upper\":6.0},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"The Electoral Act 2022 is a real institutional reform that aimed to improve electoral integrity and reduce money politics.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"Buhari's pledge to oversee a peaceful, transparent transfer in 2023 is direct evidence of transfer-rule respect.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"His statement that the elections would be free, fair, and credible, together with praise for not pursuing a third term, supports a positive transfer-rule reading.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"The absence of a preferred successor signal is modest but relevant evidence of not trying to personalize succession.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0033\",\"explanation\":\"The Twitter suspension and subsequent legal dispute show speech control and executive pressure on online expression.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0045\",\"explanation\":\"State violence and intimidation against peaceful protesters and families of the disappeared undercut the freedom score materially.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"Police suppression of supporters of an ousted opponent shows coercive crowd control and political intimidation.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0047\",\"explanation\":\"RSF's account of army-related intimidation of journalists is serious evidence of pressure on the press under Buhari's watch.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0048\",\"explanation\":\"The ARY channel takedown and arrest show broader media coercion and state/military pressure in the political environment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0049\",\"explanation\":\"HRW's annual assessment of harassment and prosecutions of critics shows a sustained repression pattern.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Nigeria in 2022 remained a noisy competitive system with real elections, but security agencies, speech restrictions, and intimidation of journalists and protesters kept political freedom uneven. Buhari also governed within a setting marked by military influence and legacy insecurity.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mixed: the dossier contains several direct Buhari statements and actions, but many coercive acts are carried out by agencies or the wider state apparatus rather than by him personally.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.1 anti-incitement and public commitment to peaceful politics\",\"4B.2 electoral-reform and transfer-rule restraint\",\"4B.3 some institutional reform and rhetoric supporting rights\",\"4B.6 information control and speech restrictions\",\"4B.8 respect for succession and no third-term pursuit\",\"4B.10 somewhat positive succession trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.4 oversight-strengthening evidence is limited\",\"4B.5 patronage/personality-cult evidence is only partly documented\",\"4B.7 equality protection evidence is weaker than the speech and transfer record\",\"4B.9 administrative harassment is present but not exhaustively traced\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"The Electoral Act and transfer rhetoric are real positives and keep the score above the low-authoritarian range.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"Buhari's explicit rejection of a third term is a meaningful restraint on personal entrenchment.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Mostly official State House speeches and legal announcements, plus Reuters, Amnesty, RSF, and HRW. The mix is good on transfer rules and speech control, but some abuse items are broader state-environment claims rather than directly presidential orders.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Muhammadu Buhari's 2022 Nigeria is a midrange case. He made notable pro-transfer and pro-election statements, signed an Electoral Act intended to improve integrity, and rejected any third-term logic. At the same time, the year still featured Twitter restrictions, protest suppression, journalist intimidation, and broader rights abuses. That means Nigeria was not closing into authoritarianism, but political freedom remained uneven and vulnerable. The score lands in the middle because the positive transfer-rule evidence is real, yet it coexists with persistent coercion and weak protection for critics.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would ignore the Electoral Act, the no-third-term posture, and the fact that Nigeria still had meaningful though imperfect contestability.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would discount the substantial evidence of speech restrictions, protest suppression, and harassment of journalists and activists.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Mixed with modest improvement on succession, but persistent coercion kept political freedom uneven.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:PAK:16682\",\"iso3\":\"PAK\",\"ruler_id\":\"2911\",\"ruler_year_id\":16682,\"ruler_name\":\"Shehbaz Sharif\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":4.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.84,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":3.5,\"upper\":4.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"The lifting of the broadcast ban on Imran Khan's speech is a concrete pro-speech move under Sharif's government.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0043\",\"explanation\":\"Sharif's condemnation of the attack on Shifa Yousafzai and instruction to punish suspects shows a direct protective response to press intimidation.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"His direction to keep the armed forces apolitical and respectful of human rights for the elections is an affirmative transfer-rule signal.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0007\",\"explanation\":\"The accession context confirms that Sharif entered office in a constitutional transition rather than a coup or closed succession.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0044\",\"explanation\":\"The FIA's arrests over critical tweets are strong evidence of continued repression of online dissent.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0045\",\"explanation\":\"Amnesty's account of violence and arbitrary detention against protesters and families of the disappeared shows a coercive civic environment.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"Police tear-gassing and detaining supporters of the ousted PM while blocking their march to Islamabad is direct protest suppression.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0047\",\"explanation\":\"RSF's reporting on intimidation by army-related agencies shows that coercive pressure on journalists persisted under Sharif.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0048\",\"explanation\":\"The ARY off-air episode and arrest of its news director show serious media coercion and the limits of civilian control.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0049\",\"explanation\":\"HRW's annual report on harassment and prosecutions of critics confirms the broader 2022 repression pattern.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Pakistan in 2022 was a fragile parliamentary system with strong military influence and extensive legacy restrictions on speech and protest. Sharif inherited a highly constrained environment and operated within a state where civilian authority remained incomplete.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mixed and often indirect: some actions are directly attributed to Sharif, but many coercive incidents are carried out by police, the FIA, or army-related agencies rather than by him personally.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.1 constitutional accession and transition\",\"4B.3 limited speech/protest responses\",\"4B.6 media pressure and partial relief\",\"4B.8 transfer-rule and apolitical-military commitments\",\"4B.10 some positive movement on civilian transition\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.2 clean anti-entrenchment evidence is limited\",\"4B.4 oversight-strengthening evidence is weak\",\"4B.5 personality-cult evidence is not central\",\"4B.7 equality protection evidence is weak\",\"4B.9 digital-harassment evidence is present but not the main dossier focus\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"The lifted broadcast ban is a real positive and prevents the score from falling lower.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0031\",\"explanation\":\"The armed-forces neutrality instruction matters, but it is aspirational and does not outweigh the repression record.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"A mix of official State House statements and Reuters plus Amnesty, RSF, and HRW. The strongest positive evidence is direct PM rhetoric; the strongest negative evidence is rights-monitor reporting on coercion by police, FIA, and army-linked agencies.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Shehbaz Sharif's 2022 Pakistan is a constrained, mixed case. He made some clear pro-speech and pro-transfer statements, lifted one broadcast ban, and publicly instructed the military to stay apolitical. But the record is dominated by arrests over critical tweets, protest suppression, journalist intimidation, and media coercion by state and army-linked agencies. That is not a fully closed system, but it is far from a robustly free one. The score sits in the lower-middle range because Sharif displayed some restraint while presiding over a security state that continued to narrow political freedom.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would miss the real, if limited, pro-speech and anti-third-term signals that separate Sharif from the hardest authoritarian cases.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would ignore the persistent coercion by the FIA, police, and army-related agencies that meaningfully constrained dissent and media freedom.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Mixed and constrained: some civilian-restraint signals existed, but coercive institutions still dominated the civic space.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:PHL:16194\",\"iso3\":\"PHL\",\"ruler_id\":\"2844\",\"ruler_year_id\":16194,\"ruler_name\":\"Bongbong Marcos\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":6.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.82,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":5.5,\"upper\":6.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0001\",\"explanation\":\"The formal inauguration and term setup confirm a standard constitutional transfer rather than a closed or irregular accession.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"The inaugural messaging and early administration tone did not signal formal censorship or suspension of political competition.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0045\",\"explanation\":\"The administration was pushed to respond to journalist threats and the Percy Lapid killing, which indicates contestation and civic pressure still operated.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0039\",\"explanation\":\"The Reuters Institute report describes campaign behavior that downgraded journalist access and favored friendly outlets, which is a real restriction on media fairness.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0041\",\"explanation\":\"Selective access and staff interference with interview requests show campaign-level narrative control.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0043\",\"explanation\":\"The Rappler incident documents physical obstruction of a journalist by campaign staff and security during the campaign.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0044\",\"explanation\":\"Online attacks by supporters and manipulated access to reporters show a hostile information environment around the campaign.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"Marcos's campaign benefited from disinformation and historical revisionism, which weakened the quality of political choice.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"The Philippines entered 2022 with competitive elections and a constitutional six-year single-term presidency. Marcos inherited a democratic framework, but campaign information quality and media access were already under strain.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mixed: many of the strongest negative items are campaign and supporter behavior rather than formal state repression, while the positive items are mostly institutional baseline and constitutional constraints.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.1 standard contestability through constitutional accession\",\"4B.3 campaign-period criticism and opposition access issues\",\"4B.6 media-freedom frictions in campaign and early office\",\"4B.8 transfer-rule baseline\",\"4B.10 relatively resilient democratic trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.2 formal entrenchment evidence is limited\",\"4B.4 oversight strengthening is absent\",\"4B.5 personality-cult evidence is more campaign-oriented than institutional\",\"4B.7 equality protection evidence is not the main issue\",\"4B.9 digital controls are indirect and campaign-based\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0001\",\"explanation\":\"A normal inauguration and single-term presidency keep the score away from the authoritarian range.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0045\",\"explanation\":\"Public pressure to address journalist attacks shows that media and accountability mechanisms still functioned.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Mostly campaign-era investigative reporting and rights monitoring, plus the constitutional baseline. The evidence is strong on campaign media pressure but less direct on post-inauguration state repression.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Bongbong Marcos's 2022 Philippines looks like a competitive democracy with meaningful but not regime-breaking campaign distortion. The election and inauguration followed ordinary constitutional rules, and the government was still answerable to public criticism after threats and a high-profile journalist killing. But the campaign itself was marked by selective access, supporter harassment, and a disinformation advantage that degraded the quality of choice. That combination supports a mid-high score: not authoritarian, but not cleanly liberal either.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would overstate the degree of closure. The Philippines still ran a normal constitutional transfer and retained public criticism and accountability pressure.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would ignore the campaign-period media manipulation, supporter harassment, and disinformation advantage that materially weakened political freedom.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Broadly resilient but campaign-distorted: formal democracy remained intact, while media access and information quality were degraded.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"iso3\":\"RUS\",\"ruler_id\":\"2759\",\"ruler_year_id\":15706,\"ruler_name\":\"Vladimir Putin\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":1.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.99,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":1.0,\"upper\":1.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0057\",\"explanation\":\"The term-limit reset is a formal baseline that explains Putin's long horizon of control; it is not a positive freedom signal, but it is necessary context for the entrenchment judgment.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0049\",\"explanation\":\"The March anti-war crackdown with mass arrests and media bans is one of the clearest signs of systematic political closure in the batch.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0050\",\"explanation\":\"Criminalizing independent war reporting and anti-war protest with severe penalties is decisive evidence of speech suppression.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0051\",\"explanation\":\"Blocking Facebook in retaliation for platform restrictions on Russian state media shows direct state information control.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0052\",\"explanation\":\"The court ban on Facebook and Instagram further deepened the blockade on independent information channels.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0053\",\"explanation\":\"The shutdown of Novaya Gazeta removes one of the last independent media outlets, sharply narrowing public space.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0054\",\"explanation\":\"Revoking Novaya Gazeta's online license only days later confirms sustained, not incidental, censorship pressure.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0055\",\"explanation\":\"The foreign-agent law signed by Putin widens tools for excluding critics from civic and political activity.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"Golos's statement that the 2022 elections could not be recognized as free is strong domestic monitoring evidence of closure.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Russia in 2022 was already a heavily centralized authoritarian system. Putin's rule combined formal electoral rituals with systematic media control, legal exclusion of critics, and suppression of anti-war expression after the invasion of Ukraine.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Direct and strong: the record includes Putin's own law-signing and a comprehensive pattern of state repression carried out by regulatory, judicial, and security organs under his control.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.2 entrenchment and legal redesign\",\"4B.3 suppression of opposition, media, and protest\",\"4B.5 loyalist and foreign-agent politics\",\"4B.6 censorship and blockade of information space\",\"4B.8 succession rule capture\",\"4B.9 administrative and digital harassment\",\"4B.10 maximal authoritarian trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.1 contestability is mostly formal and hollow\",\"4B.4 independent oversight exists only nominally\",\"4B.7 equality protection is not meaningfully operative\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"The existence of an election-monitoring statement and formal voting rituals shows the regime preserves some procedural shell, but not real freedom.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0057\",\"explanation\":\"The term-limit reset is institutional context rather than a mitigating freedom feature.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"A dense mix of Reuters, HRW, Amnesty, CPJ, Golos, EPDE, and Council of Europe reporting. The evidence is highly convergent and directly documents systemic closure across speech, media, elections, and civil society.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Vladimir Putin's 2022 Russia is the clearest full-closure case in the batch. Independent media were blocked or shut down, anti-war speech was criminalized, critics were designated foreign agents, and domestic election monitoring judged the elections not truly free. The evidence shows not just pressure but a coordinated system of legal, regulatory, and coercive closure. That warrants the floor of the scale for systematic elimination of meaningful political choice and independent constraint.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score is not available in practical terms; the dossier already shows complete political closure at the bottom of the chapter scale.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would be flatly inconsistent with the evidence of mass arrests, media bans, foreign-agent expansion, and a hollow electoral process.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Maximally authoritarian: the year deepened censorship, legal exclusion, and anti-war repression.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:THA:19649\",\"iso3\":\"THA\",\"ruler_id\":\"3414\",\"ruler_year_id\":19649,\"ruler_name\":\"Prayut Chan-o-cha\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":4.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.85,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":3.5,\"upper\":4.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"The Bangkok governor election shows real contestation and a meaningful voter choice, which is a positive signal against full closure.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"The court suspension over the term limit shows that legal checks could still bite, even if only temporarily.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0043\",\"explanation\":\"Prayut's continued defense-minister role after suspension is a real sign of partial constraint rather than total personal control.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0044\",\"explanation\":\"The court ultimately allowed him to stay on as PM, which shows a legal process rather than a purely arbitrary seizure of power.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0045\",\"explanation\":\"Wide self-censorship and legal chill in media remain major constraints on political freedom.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"The Thai Journalists Association report shows that legal chilling effects persisted even with a modest ranking improvement.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0047\",\"explanation\":\"Government warnings about monarchy-related content reflect continued speech sensitivity and pressure.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0049\",\"explanation\":\"Appointment reshuffles shaped by political considerations point to politicization of security institutions.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0050\",\"explanation\":\"Rubber bullets and arrests at the APEC protest show coercive crowd control and suppression of dissent.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0051\",\"explanation\":\"Amnesty's account of injuries and arrests at the APEC protest reinforces the negative protest-freedom picture.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Thailand in 2022 was shaped by a military-backed constitutional order and a long history of managed politics. Prayut governed within a system that still allowed some competition, but the legacy of post-coup institutional design and speech restraint remained powerful.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mixed: some evidence is directly tied to Prayut's own tenure and court battle, while much of the repression is carried out by police, regulators, and military-linked institutions rather than by him personally ordering every action.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.1 contestability\",\"4B.3 protest and media space\",\"4B.4 term-limit and judicial constraint\",\"4B.6 media chill and legal pressure\",\"4B.8 succession-rule testing\",\"4B.9 administrative harassment\",\"4B.10 mixed but erosive trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.2 clean anti-entrenchment evidence is limited because the system itself already favored incumbency\",\"4B.5 loyalty-test/personality-cult evidence is present but secondary\",\"4B.7 equality protection evidence is not the main driver\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"The Bangkok governor election shows the regime was not fully closed and keeps the score above the authoritarian floor.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0044\",\"explanation\":\"Judicial review of the term limit is a real institutional check, even though it ultimately favored Prayut.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Mostly Reuters, Freedom House, RSF, and Amnesty plus Thai journalist and policy analysis sources. The mix is good on both contestation and repression, with clear evidence that the system was neither free nor fully closed.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Prayut Chan-o-cha's 2022 Thailand is a classic mixed-authoritarian case. Elections and court review still existed, and the Bangkok governor race demonstrated real contestation. But media self-censorship, monarchy-related speech pressure, politicized reshuffles, and protest crackdowns all show that freedom was heavily constrained. The score sits at the lower middle because the system retained meaningful competition without shedding its deep post-coup controls.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would overstate closure: the governor election and court review demonstrate real, if constrained, political competition.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would underplay persistent media chill, protest repression, and incumbency advantages built into the post-coup order.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Mixed and constrained: contestation survived, but speech and protest remained heavily policed.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:TUR:15972\",\"iso3\":\"TUR\",\"ruler_id\":\"2813\",\"ruler_year_id\":15972,\"ruler_name\":\"Recep Tayyip Erdoğan\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":3.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.88,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":3.0,\"upper\":4.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0045\",\"explanation\":\"The report on legal treatment of SOGIESC rights confirms that some rights remained formally legal and that the state did not totalize every aspect of identity politics.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"The Venice Commission's review of the disinformation draft shows that the draft was still contested within legal institutions, indicating some external constraint.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0034\",\"explanation\":\"The electoral-law change lowered thresholds and exempted the president from campaign-spending constraints, which is a classic entrenchment move.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0036\",\"explanation\":\"The convictions of opposition figures and disregard of ECHR rulings show judicial repression aimed at rivals.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"HRW's report on targeting critics and controlling media/judiciary is broad evidence of authoritarian governance.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"The sharp rise in jailed journalists after Kurdish arrests shows sustained press repression.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0039\",\"explanation\":\"The new disinformation law tightened control over speech and created real jail risks for reposting content.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0041\",\"explanation\":\"Police use of tear gas and detentions against demonstrators, plus activist arrests, shows direct coercion of protest.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0042\",\"explanation\":\"The Kavala life sentence case is strong evidence of courts operating under presidential pressure.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0043\",\"explanation\":\"The EU statement on the Kavala ruling reinforces that the prosecution was seen as silencing dissent rather than ordinary justice.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0044\",\"explanation\":\"The Gülşen arrest shows continued use of prosecution and detention to police speech and identity politics.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Turkey in 2022 remained an electoral system with formal competition, but one shaped by entrenched executive dominance, politicized courts, and a highly constrained media environment. Erdoğan's power was meaningful and increasingly illiberal, though not fully identical to a closed one-party system.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Strong but not always direct: some evidence is Erdoğan's own political architecture and party-backed lawmaking, while other evidence reflects courts, police, and prosecutors operating within his dominant environment.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.2 rule manipulation and campaign advantage\",\"4B.3 repression of opposition and civil society\",\"4B.6 media and speech control\",\"4B.8 entrenchment and transfer concerns\",\"4B.9 protest policing and legal pressure\",\"4B.10 clearly erosive trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.1 contestability remains via elections, so the system is not fully closed\",\"4B.4 oversight-strengthening evidence is absent\",\"4B.5 personality-cult evidence exists but is not the central mechanism\",\"4B.7 equality protection evidence is selective and imperfect\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0045\",\"explanation\":\"Some legally protected rights remain, which keeps Turkey above the fully closed authoritarian floor.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0040\",\"explanation\":\"External legal scrutiny still mattered, though the government did not fully comply with it.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"A mix of Freedom House, HRW, Amnesty, the Venice Commission, the U.S. government, and Reuters. The evidence is strong and varied, with an especially clear pattern of rule manipulation, media control, and judicial repression.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Recep Tayyip Erdoğan's 2022 Turkey is a strongly erosive but not fully closed case. The year combined a pro-incumbent electoral-law change, politically charged prosecutions, media controls, a disinformation law, and protest policing. Yet formal elections and some legal constraints still existed, and external institutions like the Venice Commission could still register objections. That keeps the score below the fully authoritarian floor but well below the freedom-preserving middle. The evidence supports a low score because Erdoğan's system consistently narrowed contestability and punished critics while leaving a procedural shell in place.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would overstate closure because Turkey still had elections, legal review, and some rights protection, however weakened.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would understate the combined effect of electoral manipulation, judicial repression, media control, and protest policing in 2022.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Erosive authoritarianism: procedural democracy persisted, but executive dominance and censorship deepened.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:USA:15810\",\"iso3\":\"USA\",\"ruler_id\":\"2782\",\"ruler_year_id\":15810,\"ruler_name\":\"Joe Biden\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":8.0,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.9,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":7.5,\"upper\":8.5},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0043\",\"explanation\":\"Biden's January 6 remarks are direct self-binding rhetoric against anti-democratic violence and transfer disruption.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0044\",\"explanation\":\"His democracy speech directly condemns political violence and intimidation of election workers, reinforcing civic contestability.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0045\",\"explanation\":\"The policing executive order shows concrete federal restraint tools and accountability mechanisms rather than arbitrary force.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0046\",\"explanation\":\"The Uvalde response shows direct executive action against gun violence, a domestic safety issue relevant to public freedom.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0051\",\"explanation\":\"The hate-motivated violence summit and announced actions are affirmative protections for vulnerable groups.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0053\",\"explanation\":\"Nationwide DOJ outreach to combat hate is a concrete federal effort to protect political and social equality.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0054\",\"explanation\":\"The Respect for Marriage Act is direct equality-expanding legislation that Biden signed into law.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0052\",\"explanation\":\"Election threats and intimidation were real enough to require a DOJ task force, showing the presence of pressure even in a strong democracy.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0055\",\"explanation\":\"The hate-crime statistics remind us that substantial abuse remained despite federal action, limiting any claim of perfect protection.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"The United States in 2022 retained strong constitutional checks, competitive elections, and independent institutions. Biden governed within a resilient democratic structure and used executive authority largely to reinforce rather than override it.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Mostly direct at the presidential and executive-branch level, with some evidence reflecting broader institutional action under his administration rather than personal daily control.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.1 contestability and transfer norms\",\"4B.3 condemnation of violence and protection of political participation\",\"4B.4 executive restraint and accountability tools\",\"4B.7 protection for vulnerable groups and equality\",\"4B.8 transfer-rule reaffirmation\",\"4B.10 positive trajectory under pressure\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.2 manipulation/entrenchment evidence is limited\",\"4B.5 personality-cult or party-capture evidence is not central\",\"4B.6 media freedom pressure is not the main dossier\",\"4B.9 surveillance/shutdown evidence is limited\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0052\",\"explanation\":\"The very need for an election-threats task force shows that intimidation existed, even though the state responded to it.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0055\",\"explanation\":\"Substantial hate-crime numbers mean the environment was not benign, only resilient enough to respond.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Mostly primary presidential transcripts and DOJ/White House materials, with one retrospective DOJ statistical summary. The mix is strong on direct presidential action and institutional response, though it is naturally lighter on adversarial criticism because the U.S. is not an authoritarian case.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Joe Biden's 2022 United States is a high-freedom case with strong institutional resilience. He repeatedly condemned anti-democratic violence, directed federal policing and hate-crime prevention measures, signed equality-expanding legislation, and did not threaten transfer rules. The remaining weaknesses are real but limited: election intimidation existed, hate crimes remained substantial, and the government had to build response mechanisms. That keeps the score high but not perfect, because the record shows a durable democracy managing threats rather than an exceptional expansion of freedom under severe pressure.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would ignore the strength of the constitutional order, the direct federal restraint tools, and the absence of any serious transfer crisis in 2022.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would overstate the record: there were still real threats to election workers and hate-crime harms that required active government response.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"High and resilient: Biden used federal power to reinforce pluralism and protect vulnerable groups within a strong democratic baseline.\"}]},{\"dossier_job_key\":\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:VNM:19647\",\"iso3\":\"VNM\",\"ruler_id\":\"3411\",\"ruler_year_id\":19647,\"ruler_name\":\"Nguyễn Phú Trọng\",\"period_start_year\":2022,\"period_end_year\":2022,\"chapter_id\":\"4B\",\"rubric_version\":\"chapter_4b_v3\",\"calibration_batch_id\":\"chapter-judge:2022-top20-hybrid-v2-judges-v1:2022:4B\",\"calibrated_against\":[\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:RUS:15706\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:DEU:16984\",\"dossier:2022-top20-hybrid-v2:2022:IND:19645\"],\"score_1_to_10\":1.5,\"insufficient_evidence_reason\":null,\"confidence_score\":0.97,\"plausible_score_range\":{\"lower\":1.0,\"upper\":2.0},\"decisive_positive_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0002\",\"explanation\":\"The biography clarifies Trọng's formal party-centered authority, which is important context for responsibility but not a freedom-positive fact.\"}],\"decisive_negative_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0020\",\"explanation\":\"The State Department describes Vietnam as an authoritarian one-party state with arbitrary detention, censorship, and severe assembly limits.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0022\",\"explanation\":\"HRW documents house arrest and short-term detention used to stop activists from attending protests, trials, and meetings, showing direct restriction of political activity.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0025\",\"explanation\":\"Amnesty's 2022 crackdown summary gives specific detention counts and repression of journalists, activists, and religious practitioners.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0027\",\"explanation\":\"Freedom House confirms a one-party system with tightly restricted expression, assembly, and civil society.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0030\",\"explanation\":\"RSF's summary sentencing of an independent reporter for anti-state propaganda is direct evidence of speech repression.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0035\",\"explanation\":\"The internet-freedom report documents platform takedowns, tighter content rules, and privacy concerns from state data controls.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"Reuters' report on onshore data storage and local offices shows additional state leverage over information access.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0038\",\"explanation\":\"Rules limiting which social-media accounts can post news-related content show continued tightening of political speech controls.\"}],\"inherited_baseline_and_constraints\":\"Vietnam in 2022 was a one-party authoritarian system led by the Communist Party of Vietnam. Trọng's formal power was party-centered and the state already tightly constrained opposition, media, and civic activity.\",\"ruler_attribution\":\"Direct at the system level and indirect personally: the dossier attributes repression to the party-state led by Trọng, though individual orders are often not traceable to him personally.\",\"supported_lenses\":[\"4B.2 party control and censorship\",\"4B.3 repression of activists and journalists\",\"4B.6 media and internet control\",\"4B.7 suppression of political equality and opposition\",\"4B.9 digital controls and administrative restrictions\",\"4B.10 deeply negative trajectory\"],\"missing_or_weak_lenses\":[\"4B.1 free competition is effectively absent\",\"4B.4 independent oversight strengthening is absent\",\"4B.5 loyalty tests are more systemic than personal in the dossier\",\"4B.8 transfer-rule evidence is not the core issue in a one-party state\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0002\",\"explanation\":\"The party-centered biography helps define authority, but it does not mitigate the repression record.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0037\",\"explanation\":\"Regulation of social media is framed as governance, yet the practical effect is still coercive control of information.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"A mix of U.S. government, HRW, Amnesty, Freedom House, RSF, and Reuters. The evidence is broad and mutually reinforcing on one-party control, with direct personal attribution often mediated through the party-state rather than a signed Trọng order.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Structured local priors were not collected in this experiment; the judge relied entirely on the embedded projection and its cited evidence.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Nguyễn Phú Trọng's 2022 Vietnam is a highly closed one-party case. The year was marked by arrests, house arrests, censorship, online-content restrictions, and broader controls on political activity and media. The evidence is not always a direct personal order from Trọng, but the party-state he leads is clearly responsible for the political environment. The score therefore sits near the floor because real political choice and independent constraint are largely absent, even if the dossier sometimes captures the system more than the individual.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A lower score would be redundant with the chapter floor, and the record already captures near-total closure rather than a separably lower state.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A higher score would be incompatible with the one-party structure, the repression of critics, and the extensive digital and media controls documented in 2022.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"trajectory\",\"value\":\"Severely authoritarian and stable: the party-state continued to dominate elections, media, and civic space.\"}]}],\"unavailable_dossiers\":[],\"batch_notes\":[\"Low anchors in this batch are Russia, China, Iran, Vietnam, and Egypt; high anchors are Germany, Japan, and the United States.\",\"Mixed cases were scored on the actual practice of contestability, opposition space, media freedom, and transfer rules, not on incident counts alone.\",\"No dossiers were unavailable; all judgments rely only on the embedded projections and cited dossier evidence.\"],\"run_profile\":{\"provider_profile\":\"gpt-5.4-mini\",\"provider\":\"openai\",\"model\":\"gpt-5.4-mini\",\"dossier_count\":20,\"unavailable_dossier_count\":0,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":\"unknown_not_exposed_by_tool\",\"output_tokens\":\"unknown_not_exposed_by_tool\",\"total_tokens\":\"unknown_not_exposed_by_tool\",\"estimated_cost_usd\":\"unknown_not_exposed_by_tool\"}}}"}}
