\",\"8B.10\"],\"contrary_evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"E0165\",\"explanation\":\"The OECD's downturn analysis shows the war decision imposed major strategic and economic costs.\"},{\"evidence_id\":\"E0175\",\"explanation\":\"Discovery-only evidence on university places was not used as decisive support, leaving the educational side of the portfolio thinner than the macro record.\"}],\"source_mix\":\"Mix of primary government records, multilateral assessments, and Reuters/major-media corroboration; official self-reporting was treated as program and implementation evidence, not as independent proof of success.\",\"structured_prior_summary\":\"Local priors indicate a low-accountability, weak-government-effectiveness baseline (V-Dem accountability -0.61; rule of law 0.171; WGI government effectiveness -0.695), used only as context.\",\"chapter_rationale\":\"Putin's 2022 record is low on competence because the core strategic choice produced a severe shock that outweighed the regime's capacity to stabilize around it. The dossier does show real mobilization, emergency financial control, and formal budget execution, so this is not a collapse of state action. But the larger picture is a self-inflicted war shock, sanctions pressure, and a weak medium-term outlook, which leaves the year-end position worse rather than better on the ruler's own strategic terms. Moral approval or disapproval of the war did not affect the score; on execution and progress, this is a low score but not a null case because command and mobilization were real.\",\"lower_anchor_rejected\":\"A 3 would understate the extent of command, emergency stabilization, and formal execution visible in 2022.\",\"higher_anchor_rejected\":\"A 5 would overstate the degree to which the regime's strategic objective was advanced rather than damaged by the 2022 choice and its consequences.\",\"manual_review_required\":false,\"manual_review_reason_type\":null,\"manual_review_reason\":null,\"chapter_specific\":[{\"field\":\"p